/* Industry module: lessons, cards and lesson quizzes. Questions follow the Red Book (1st ed.) order; answers are IBZY study notes. */
window.IBZY_CONTENT["industry"] = {"units": {"ind-u1": [{"id": "ind-u1-l1", "title": "TMT business models & valuation", "pages": "167–171", "cards": [{"id": "ind-u1-1", "q": "What are the most common types of business models in the telecom and media industry?", "a": "<ul><li><b>Traditional equipment sales / wireless services:</b> telecom equipment, networking products and services like broadband and cable TV plans. The oldest TMT segment, with very low growth — revenue is one-off equipment sales plus monthly plans, so growth depends on geographic expansion, which has stalled. In return: high operating leverage, high barriers to entry and few competitors (hence antitrust concerns). Incumbents include AT&amp;T, Verizon and T-Mobile.</li><li><b>Subscription streaming:</b> Netflix, Hulu, Spotify. Built on acquiring customers, minimising churn and raising prices by offering more value than rivals. User growth comes first; many have no clear route to profit before reaching big scale. One of the most competitive spaces anywhere, having disrupted cable and radio.</li><li><b>Advertising:</b> Facebook (Meta), Twitter (X), Google — judged on MAU/DAU and engagement. Strong growth, but the big risk is regulators restricting data collection and trying to break up big tech.</li><li><b>Media networks / diversified entertainment:</b> conglomerates that create, buy and distribute content (Disney, NBCUniversal, ViacomCBS — now Paramount) and earn from ads, affiliate fees, subscriptions, product sales and licensing.</li></ul><p>Telecom and media are mature, so they’re usually valued with traditional methods; early-stage SaaS has its own KPI-based metrics (covered in the SaaS unit).</p>", "p": 167}, {"id": "ind-u1-2", "q": "What are a few notable trends going on in the TMT industry right now?", "a": "<p>The book’s list (written in 2020):</p><ul><li><b>Convergence of business models:</b> companies expanding into other segments organically or via M&amp;A — Google into subscriptions, hardware and fitness (Fitbit); Facebook into social commerce, creator tools and livestreaming; even slow-growth AT&amp;T into streaming.</li><li><b>Scale-driven M&amp;A:</b> mergers of equals in low-growth industries — CBS/Viacom, AT&amp;T/Time Warner, T-Mobile/Sprint — chasing leadership, cost synergies, operating leverage and diversification.</li><li><b>The “streaming wars”:</b> COVID accelerated streaming, and new entrants piled in (Disney+, Apple TV+, HBO Max, Peacock, Quibi).</li><li><b>Hosted online events:</b> lockdowns pushed concerts and events online, boosting esports and livestreaming.</li><li><b>5G and edge computing:</b> high-growth segments led by enterprise and B2B partnerships (e.g. AT&amp;T–Microsoft). 5G needs edge infrastructure, and edge computing needs 5G applications.</li><li><b>AI assistants:</b> rising consumer adoption despite security and privacy concerns.</li></ul><p class=\"muted\">Update before interviews: Quibi shut down in late 2020; AT&amp;T has since spun off WarnerMedia (now Warner Bros. Discovery) and much of DirecTV; and generative AI is now the dominant TMT theme.</p>", "p": 168}, {"id": "ind-u1-3", "q": "Which valuation metrics are common to see for traditional telecom companies?", "a": "<p>Traditional telecom — mobile carriers, convergent providers, cable operators, data centres/infrastructure — is capital-intensive with high fixed costs, usually financed with debt (it’s concentrated, mature and not very cyclical). So these companies have large interest expense and heavy depreciation, plus tax incentives for R&amp;D (e.g. the 5G build-out). That makes accrual earnings volatile, so <b>P/E is less useful</b>.</p><ul><li><b>EV/EBITDA</b> is the main multiple, often on a run-rate or normalised multi-year basis.</li><li><b>EV/(EBITDA − Capex)</b> is common too — a better proxy for operating FCF given how capital-intensive the business is.</li><li>Look at both <b>LTM and NTM</b> multiples to understand valuation trends.</li><li>Industry-specific multiples: EV/data centres, EV/net PP&amp;E, EV/route miles, EV/fiber miles, EV/access lines, EV/broadband subscribers, EV/broadcast revenue.</li></ul><p>Since many telecoms are diversified, a <b>sum-of-the-parts</b> valuation may be needed.</p>", "p": 169}, {"id": "ind-u1-4", "q": "What metrics would you use to measure user engagement?", "a": "<p>Engagement is how involved users are with a product (site, app, platform). High engagement means users get value, which drives retention and recurring revenue — critical for media companies.</p><ul><li>Daily and monthly active users (DAU / MAU)</li><li>Number of active (paying) subscribers</li><li>Average daily or weekly time in the app</li><li>Page views / site visits</li><li>Churn rate (or retention %)</li><li>Conversion rate from free to paid plans</li></ul>", "p": 169}, {"id": "ind-u1-5", "q": "Which multiples are most commonly used to value modern media companies?", "a": "<p>High-growth media companies are often unprofitable or barely profitable — acquiring customers inevitably means big losses — so cash-flow and profit multiples miss their value.</p><p><b>EV/Revenue</b> is common, but less useful if the company is prioritising user growth over monetisation. The real value drivers are user count, new customer growth and churn, so <b>KPI multiples</b> like EV/MAU, EV/DAU and EV/subscribers often give a better read.</p>", "p": 169}, {"id": "ind-u1-6", "q": "For a company with a product meant for high frequency in usage, what is one way to assess user engagement?", "a": "<p>The <b>DAU/MAU ratio</b> (“stickiness”): the share of monthly users who use the product on a given day.</p><div class=\"formula\">DAU/MAU = Daily active users ÷ Monthly active users × 100</div><p>500 DAU and 1,000 MAU → 50%: the average user is active about 15 days in a 30-day month. Sequoia puts a typical ratio at 10–20%; apps like WhatsApp can top 50%.</p><p>It only works for products meant for daily use (social, messaging) — useful for Facebook or Snapchat, not for Airbnb or Uber.</p>", "p": 170}, {"id": "ind-u1-7", "q": "What are economies of scale and could you give me an example?", "a": "<p>Economies of scale mean per-unit costs fall as output rises, because costs are spread over more units — so margins improve.</p><p>Example: <b>Apple</b>. Selling millions of iPhones a quarter lets it commit to huge component orders, giving it the negotiating leverage to win volume discounts and a lower average cost per unit.</p>", "p": 170}, {"id": "ind-u1-8", "q": "What effect does having high operating leverage have on the scalability of a business?", "a": "<p>Operating leverage is how much of a company’s cost base is <b>fixed</b> rather than variable.</p><p><b>High operating leverage:</b> once fixed costs (rent, utilities, infrastructure) are covered, each extra dollar of revenue is earned at a much higher profit, since those costs don’t rise with output. Very scalable.</p><p><b>Low operating leverage:</b> mostly variable costs, which rise in step with revenue, so each extra dollar adds less profit.</p>", "p": 170}, {"id": "ind-u1-9", "q": "Can you give me an example of a company benefiting from operating leverage?", "a": "<p>A <b>telecom</b> that has finished building its network. The build needs huge upfront capex — equipment, construction, security. But once it’s running, adding a customer costs very little; most ongoing costs are maintenance. The initial investment is eventually earned back, leaving a high-margin business with recurring revenue and outsized profit on each extra customer.</p>", "p": 170}, {"id": "ind-u1-10", "q": "When might having high operating leverage not benefit a company?", "a": "<p>When sales come in <b>below</b> expectations. With a high fixed-cost base and a big upfront investment, the break-even point is higher, so there’s more business risk — weak volume can be ruinous. A company with mostly variable costs can cut costs as sales fall; one with fixed costs has very little flexibility.</p>", "p": 171}]}, {"id": "ind-u1-l2", "title": "Moats, trends & 5G", "pages": "171–173", "cards": [{"id": "ind-u1-11", "q": "What are network effects and can you provide some examples?", "a": "<p>A network effect means a product becomes more valuable with each additional user. Once it hits critical mass, growth compounds — new users come with less effort and spending.</p><ul><li><b>Facebook:</b> as its user base and engagement grew, it became the place advertisers had to be, building a durable moat in advertising and opening room for new products.</li><li><b>Google Search:</b> more users improve the results, making the product better for everyone and attracting more advertisers — a big reason for its ~90% share of search (the book says ~87%).</li></ul>", "p": 171}, {"id": "ind-u1-12", "q": "What is pricing power and how would you measure it?", "a": "<p>There’s no formula, but a useful test is: if the company raised prices by ~25%, what would happen to retention? If customers mostly stay, it has pricing power — it can raise prices without a big jump in churn.</p><p>Pricing power depends on how essential the product is, how unique its value is, and how few alternatives there are. It usually comes from a differentiated product that users see as essential and unique.</p>", "p": 171}, {"id": "ind-u1-13", "q": "What is the difference between a cyclical and secular trend?", "a": "<p><b>Cyclical:</b> tied to the economic cycle — less predictable in timing than seasonality, near impossible to time, and potentially more damaging to results. E.g. <b>semiconductors</b>, driven by GDP growth and enterprise spending.</p><p><b>Secular:</b> a long-lasting, often permanent shift driven by new technology or changing consumer behaviour. E.g. Netflix riding the shift to over-the-top streaming. These shifts are usually gradual, giving companies time to adapt — yet many ignore them until they become obsolete (Blockbuster).</p>", "p": 171}, {"id": "ind-u1-14", "q": "What are some examples of barriers to entry that can help protect a company's profit margins?", "a": "<ul><li><b>Network effects:</b> once a platform reaches critical mass, it’s very hard to take share from it.</li><li><b>Economies of scale:</b> incumbents’ lower unit costs mean higher profits to reinvest, while new entrants start at a cost disadvantage.</li><li><b>Proprietary technology:</b> a unique offering (especially patented) leaves little or no competition.</li><li><b>High capex requirements:</b> the cost of infrastructure, machinery and R&amp;D deters entrants.</li><li><b>Switching costs:</b> unless a rival is much better, switching isn’t worth it.</li><li><b>Regulatory hurdles:</b> approval requirements in regulated sectors (e.g. healthcare) keep entrants out.</li></ul><p>Sustained long-term profits need differentiation in an industry with high barriers to entry.</p>", "p": 172}, {"id": "ind-u1-15", "q": "What is 5G, and what benefits will it provide?", "a": "<p>The next-generation mobile network — far faster than 4G at full capacity, with lower latency and fewer bandwidth bottlenecks. It promises to accelerate smart cities, autonomous vehicles, drones, VR, transport, manufacturing, agriculture, healthcare and more. Peak speeds are claimed at up to ~100x 4G.</p><p>The book (2020) noted that the rollout was slower and patchier than advertised — many “5G” users were on slower tiers, and outside major cities the step-up from LTE was barely noticeable. Like most new technology, full deployment takes years.</p>", "p": 172}, {"id": "ind-u1-16", "q": "The term “latency” often comes up when discussing 5G. What does it mean?", "a": "<p>The <b>delay</b> in transmitting data between devices across a network. High latency hurts the user experience — like being stuck in traffic. Latency is closely linked to (but not the same as) <b>bandwidth</b>, a network’s maximum data transfer rate over a period.</p>", "p": 172}, {"id": "ind-u1-17", "q": "Which companies do you expect to benefit the most from the increased adoption of 5G?", "a": "<p><b>US wireless carriers:</b> AT&amp;T, T-Mobile and Verizon, given their combined dominance of the market.</p><p><b>Semiconductor companies:</b> Qualcomm (5G modem and infrastructure platforms), Broadcom (a leading supplier of 5G smartphone chips) and Qorvo (radio frequency solutions).</p>", "p": 173}, {"id": "ind-u1-18", "q": "Many enterprises are expected to test private 5G deployments. What are the implications?", "a": "<p>Private 5G means dedicated radio antennas and transmission resources for a single enterprise, independent of public cellular networks. That brings bandwidth to places carriers have neglected, or sites with tight security needs. In theory it’s more secure — the enterprise sets and monitors its own security policies instead of relying on a third party — and data can be kept locally on site.</p>", "p": 173}, {"id": "ind-u1-19", "q": "\"Edge AI\" is a common buzzword when discussing artificial intelligence. What does it involve?", "a": "<p>Edge AI combines <b>edge computing</b> and AI: running machine-learning models locally on a device or nearby server instead of in a remote data centre. Too many devices on one network cause congestion; processing data on the device itself avoids that, giving immediate, real-time results with no network delay. The upside is faster, more responsive devices and a better user experience.</p>", "p": 173}]}, {"id": "ind-u1-l3", "title": "Streaming & OTT", "pages": "173–176", "cards": [{"id": "ind-u1-20", "q": "What do over-the-top services refer to and can you name a few examples of “OTT” providers?", "a": "<p>OTT means delivering film and TV over the internet, bypassing traditional cable and satellite. Examples: Netflix, Hulu, Amazon Prime Video.</p><p>It’s driven by “cord-cutters” and “cord-trimmers” dropping or downgrading cable for cheaper subscriptions. Consumers don’t want to pay for channels they never watch, and want the customisation OTT gives them.</p>", "p": 173}, {"id": "ind-u1-21", "q": "Tell me about the pricing models used in the OTT industry.", "a": "<ul><li><b>SVOD (subscription video on demand):</b> a recurring fee (usually monthly) for unlimited access to the whole library on any device — Netflix, Hulu, HBO. The most common model.</li><li><b>TVOD (transactional):</b> pay-per-view — users pay per title or package rather than for the whole catalogue — Google Play, iTunes, Amazon Prime Video rentals.</li><li><b>AVOD (advertising-supported):</b> free to the viewer, funded by ads — e.g. Hulu’s ad-supported tier alongside its subscription plans.</li></ul><p class=\"muted\">Since the book, most big streamers (Netflix, Disney+, Max) have added cheaper ad-supported tiers — a hybrid of SVOD and AVOD.</p>", "p": 173}, {"id": "ind-u1-22", "q": "What is the “varied access” pricing model?", "a": "<p>Offering different tiers of access to suit different subscribers — e.g. Netflix’s plans ranging from a single-screen option up to multi-screen plans with extras like higher resolution.</p>", "p": 174}, {"id": "ind-u1-23", "q": "What are the two categories of programming we see in OTT?", "a": "<p><b>Original content:</b> can give a big competitive edge, which is why Netflix spends heavily on originals (Stranger Things, Bird Box, House of Cards).</p><p><b>Licensed content:</b> rights to show content that aired elsewhere. Cheaper, but you have less control — e.g. Disney pulled its own and Pixar’s content from Netflix when it launched Disney+.</p>", "p": 174}, {"id": "ind-u1-24", "q": "How would you forecast the subscription revenue for Netflix?", "a": "<p>Revenue hinges on <b>churn</b> — how well Netflix keeps subscribers as rivals like Disney+ and Hulu compete.</p><ol><li><b>Paid memberships at period end:</b> mostly driven by renewal of the existing base. Netflix doesn’t disclose churn, but third-party streaming analytics can give estimates. Key question: can it keep the subscribers it gained in lockdown and stop them leaving for rivals?</li><li><b>Net paid additions:</b> historically fairly stable for a mature leader, but COVID and new competitors made this harder. Factor in price increases (modest, so likely little churn impact) and the content pipeline vs competitors. Use a wide range for churn, since it depends on how viewers react to the content.</li><li><b>Average monthly revenue per paying member:</b> based on historical trends and recent price changes — likely stable given competitors undercutting on price. Annualise it.</li><li><b>Revenue</b> = projected subscribers (net of churn) × annualised revenue per member. Repeat for each forecast year.</li></ol>", "p": 174}, {"id": "ind-u1-25", "q": "For pricing, why does Disney undercut its competitors?", "a": "<p>At launch, Disney+ cost $6.99/month vs $8.99 for Netflix’s basic plan (Netflix’s more popular plans were $13.99 and $17.99 in 2020).</p><ul><li><b>Diversification:</b> Netflix lives entirely off subscriptions; Disney is a conglomerate with the money to fund an aggressive customer-acquisition strategy. The goal was to take share first (with its own content pulled from Netflix) and raise prices later — management targeted profitability around 2024.</li><li><b>Disney+ isn’t the core business:</b> theme parks, studios and original content are. Going direct-to-consumer strengthens Disney’s relationship with customers and creates a flywheel where each offering reinforces the others — online and in person — making Disney a one-stop shop for its content.</li></ul><p class=\"muted\">Prices have since risen sharply across streaming, and Disney’s streaming business turned profitable in 2024.</p>", "p": 175}, {"id": "ind-u1-26", "q": "Disney owns Disney+, Hulu, and ESPN+. Why does Disney not just bundle them in one offering?", "a": "<p>It does offer a bundle — but many users prefer to buy services individually, and each service serves a purpose:</p><ul><li><b>Hulu:</b> taking control of Hulu, long seen as Netflix’s main rival, put Disney immediately at the centre of the streaming wars, before Disney+ was fully developed.</li><li><b>ESPN+:</b> sport is different — fans want <b>live</b> events, like traditional TV. That suits an affiliate-fee model with pay-per-view or pay-for-access pricing, and natural breaks in play create room for ads.</li></ul>", "p": 175}, {"id": "ind-u1-27", "q": "Many investors initially dismissed Roku following their IPO. Why has the company far exceeded initial expectations to date?", "a": "<p>After its IPO, many expected hardware-reliant Roku to be crushed by players like Apple. Instead, it became a leading connected-TV platform by 2020:</p><ul><li><b>Built-in OS:</b> partnering with TV makers so Roku OS comes preinstalled.</li><li><b>Simple and cheap:</b> despite a dated design, its ease of use and affordability carved out a niche.</li><li><b>Neutrality:</b> partnerships with Netflix, Hulu, Disney+ and others let users stream any major app. Unlike Amazon, Google or Apple, it isn’t pushing its own services — it just offers an easy, affordable way to stream.</li></ul>", "p": 175}, {"id": "ind-u1-28", "q": "Why has Apple shifted its focus from hardware to software?", "a": "<p>Computing hardware sales peaked around 2011 and growth has slowed since; it’s increasingly hard to persuade customers to upgrade. Hardware is periodic one-off revenue, not recurring.</p><p>Software and services offer a larger addressable market and higher retention (“stickiness”). So Apple has focused on services and deepening its <b>ecosystem</b> — App Store, iCloud, Apple Music and devices that work best together. Many products are deliberately not compatible with competitors’, so once you’re in, it’s hard to leave.</p>", "p": 176}]}, {"id": "ind-u1-l4", "title": "Software, audio & gaming", "pages": "176–178", "cards": [{"id": "ind-u1-29", "q": "What is enterprise collaboration software?", "a": "<p>Tools that let teams share information, coordinate work, manage projects and communicate. Sub-markets include communication apps (chat, video calls), file sharing and content collaboration, and project management. The book expected the market to exceed ~$18bn within a few years.</p><p>COVID made these tools central to how work gets done as companies went remote — Zoom was seen as a major beneficiary, since employers and schools had little choice but to use video conferencing.</p>", "p": 176}, {"id": "ind-u1-30", "q": "Tell me about a few of the ongoing trends in the enterprise collaboration software industry.", "a": "<ul><li><b>Platform vendors vs specialists:</b> as the market matures, suites offering many tools on one platform gain an edge.</li><li><b>Third-party integrations:</b> single-product players must integrate with other apps to compete with big vendors.</li><li><b>Consolidation:</b> a fragmented market with low barriers to entry is ripe for M&amp;A (productivity, note-taking, scheduling apps).</li><li><b>Remote work:</b> normalised in 2020, with many large companies keeping it long term.</li><li><b>Security and trust:</b> privacy and security are critical — Zoom was heavily criticised in 2020 for security flaws.</li></ul>", "p": 176}, {"id": "ind-u1-31", "q": "There are two distinct groups in enterprise collaboration software, platform vendors and specialist players. Could you tell me how they differ?", "a": "<p><b>Platform vendors</b> have a portfolio of products, a trusted brand and an existing customer base — e.g. Microsoft, which can focus on bundling Teams into Office 365 and onboarding its existing users. They can afford to offer free plans long term.</p><p><b>Specialist players</b> are fast-growing newcomers with a single product (e.g. Zoom). They must urgently win new customers and monetise them, while platform vendors simply leverage users who already know and trust them.</p>", "p": 177}, {"id": "ind-u1-32", "q": "How were Microsoft Teams able to capture market share away from Zoom?", "a": "<p>Not by being more innovative or better at winning new customers — but through its <b>existing customer base</b>. Microsoft had long-term relationships with companies that already used Office, so switching to Teams was the convenient option for them.</p>", "p": 177}, {"id": "ind-u1-33", "q": "Would you consider B2B software to be non-cyclical?", "a": "<p>Largely yes. Vista Equity’s Robert F. Smith has argued that software contracts are better than first-lien debt — companies pay their software subscriptions before their lenders.</p><p>Many B2B tools are now embedded in how companies run — removing them would stop operations. Contracts are usually long term with high switching costs. So B2B software tends to be non-cyclical and resilient in downturns.</p>", "p": 177}, {"id": "ind-u1-34", "q": "In the context of technology applications, what does product bundling mean and could you provide an example?", "a": "<p>Selling complementary products that are often bought together as a single package. Some bundles are directly complementary — <b>Adobe Creative Cloud</b> (Photoshop, Illustrator, After Effects). Others are only loosely linked — <b>Amazon Prime</b> with Prime Video, where having both adds little beyond the discount.</p>", "p": 177}, {"id": "ind-u1-35", "q": "Name a weakness in Spotify’s business model that explains its lack of profitability.", "a": "<p>Its <b>marginal costs</b>. Fixed costs (SG&amp;A, R&amp;D) are in line with peers, but Spotify pays large royalties to record labels, songwriters and publishers on its streams. Unlike Netflix, it has almost no original content, so its value proposition depends on the labels, who ultimately control the rates (though its huge user base gives it some leverage to negotiate). On top of that, competitors like Apple — with diversified revenue — could afford long free trials.</p><p class=\"muted\">Spotify reached sustained profitability in 2024 after price rises and cost cuts.</p>", "p": 177}, {"id": "ind-u1-36", "q": "Despite its low margins, why do you suppose Spotify offers so many discount pricing models on its offerings?", "a": "<p>Spotify prioritises <b>customer retention</b> over ARPU for now. Discounted plans — the popular student plan, bundles with Hulu, Showtime, Starbucks and others — cost short-term revenue but build a loyal base. Churn is high, but should fall over time, and the retained base can be sold other products (podcasts, audiobooks).</p><p>Its main competitive advantage is the size of its user base and network. The bigger it is, the more artists need to be on Spotify — and the less leverage they have in negotiations.</p>", "p": 178}, {"id": "ind-u1-37", "q": "What is demand-aggregation and could you name some companies capitalizing on this trend?", "a": "<p>Business models built on being the middleman between businesses and consumers. Examples: <b>Groupon</b> (discount deals connecting consumers with retailers and restaurants), <b>Yelp</b> (crowd-sourced reviews of businesses) and <b>Apple News+</b> (aggregating outlets into a personalised feed).</p>", "p": 178}, {"id": "ind-u1-38", "q": "Tell me about the trend of livestreaming.", "a": "<p>Consumers increasingly use live video apps to interact with friends, family and creators in real time. Livestreaming first went mainstream in South Korea, and has surged globally because people like watching events as they happen and interacting with the creator. Examples: Facebook Live, Periscope (since shut down) and Twitch.</p>", "p": 178}, {"id": "ind-u1-39", "q": "Where do the opportunities for monetization in the Esports industry lie?", "a": "<p>Four general revenue streams: <b>merchandise, sponsorships, media rights and ticket (gate) revenue</b>. Most revenue actually comes via advertising and sponsorship, which depend on audience size and <b>engagement hours</b>, not just views.</p><p>Advertisers like esports because engagement is very high, and fans trust players who stream for hours daily and build communities. The catch: the audience skews young, with less spending power, so it’s considered hard to monetise.</p>", "p": 178}]}, {"id": "ind-u1-l5", "title": "Semis, gaming & big-tech moves", "pages": "178–180", "cards": [{"id": "ind-u1-40", "q": "Why is the semiconductor industry known for being cyclical?", "a": "<p>Demand closely follows the economy (roughly GDP growth): enterprise IT spending swings with economic conditions, and consumer purchases of smartphones and laptops drop sharply in downturns.</p><p>Products also have <b>short life cycles</b> — even small improvements can make chips obsolete quickly. So the industry is prone to inventory build-ups followed by write-downs.</p>", "p": 178}, {"id": "ind-u1-41", "q": "What are the five types of virtualization?", "a": "<p>Virtualisation means running multiple virtual systems on one physical system, cutting IT costs and improving efficiency.</p><ol><li><b>Desktop:</b> a desktop OS runs as a virtual machine on a server alongside other virtual desktops.</li><li><b>Application:</b> apps are packaged and run separately from the OS, in a “sandbox”.</li><li><b>Server:</b> many virtual machines on one physical server, using it more efficiently.</li><li><b>Storage:</b> physical storage pooled to look like a single virtual device.</li><li><b>Network:</b> hardware and software combined so several physical networks are managed as one virtual network.</li></ol>", "p": 179}, {"id": "ind-u1-42", "q": "Could you name an example of virtualized distribution?", "a": "<p><b>Cloud gaming</b> (“gaming-as-a-service”): games run on remote servers and stream straight to the player’s device, instead of relying on hardware like discs or local consoles. At launch it was often likened to Netflix because the experience is similar.</p>", "p": 179}, {"id": "ind-u1-43", "q": "What impact did the success of Fortnite have on business models in the gaming industry?", "a": "<p>Fortnite was <b>free-to-play</b> yet generated well over $2bn across platforms and brought esports into the mainstream. Instead of charging upfront, Epic Games monetised through optional in-game purchases — skins, costumes, Battle Passes, V-Bucks.</p><p>Being free removed friction, so players could easily join friends, and it built a huge following fast. That audience led to sponsorships, ad revenue and live competitions. It showed incumbents how much revenue lies beyond the initial sale — esports, advertising, in-app purchases.</p>", "p": 179}, {"id": "ind-u1-44", "q": "What will the implications of Apple’s IDFA changes in iOS 14 have on advertising companies?", "a": "<p>Apple announced that from iOS 14, apps must ask users’ permission before using the <b>IDFA</b> (Identifier for Advertisers), Apple’s mobile ad ID used to track users across apps and sites. This undercut a key part of in-app advertising — targeting and measuring ads. Most users were expected to decline.</p><p>Apple delayed it to early 2021 (launched as App Tracking Transparency) to give developers time to adjust. Ad-dependent companies like Facebook warned it would hurt their ad revenue.</p><p class=\"muted\">It did — Meta later estimated a ~$10bn hit to 2022 revenue.</p>", "p": 179}, {"id": "ind-u1-45", "q": "What did Apple announce in 2020 that will negatively impact Intel?", "a": "<p>Apple announced it would stop using Intel processors in Macs and move to its own custom ARM-based chips (“Apple Silicon”), promising better performance, energy efficiency and battery life. The first Apple Silicon Mac shipped at the end of 2020, with the transition expected to take about two years.</p><p class=\"muted\">The transition finished in 2023.</p>", "p": 180}]}, {"id": "ind-u1-l6", "title": "Telecom deals, regulation & media trends", "pages": "180–181", "cards": [{"id": "ind-u1-46", "q": "Tell me about the controversial T-Mobile and Sprint merger that brought up concerns of a monopoly and how it ended up being approved.", "a": "<p>Announced in 2018 and completed in 2020 after long regulatory scrutiny, the merger cut the number of major US carriers from <b>four to three</b>.</p><p>To get it approved, T-Mobile agreed not to raise prices for three years (until 2023). The Justice Department also required divestitures to <b>DISH Network</b> — Sprint’s prepaid brands (e.g. Boost Mobile), spectrum and many retail stores — so DISH could become a viable fourth carrier and preserve competition.</p>", "p": 180}, {"id": "ind-u1-47", "q": "How have the acquisitions of Time Warner and DirectTV by AT&T performed to date?", "a": "<p>Poorly. AT&amp;T bought <b>DirecTV</b> in 2015 for ~$49bn, which looked strategic as online video boomed, then completed the controversial <b>Time Warner</b> merger in 2018.</p><p>The vision: use DirecTV’s subscribers as leverage with programmers, then add Time Warner and HBO’s original content to build the most complete premium offering. In practice, AT&amp;T ended up with a confusing set of overlapping streaming brands, carried over $150bn of debt by the end of 2019 (passed on to customers via higher prices), and lost both traditional TV and streaming subscribers.</p><p class=\"muted\">Since the book: AT&amp;T spun off WarnerMedia, which merged with Discovery in 2022, and moved DirecTV into a joint venture with TPG in 2021 — effectively unwinding both deals.</p>", "p": 180}, {"id": "ind-u1-48", "q": "What is the FCC agency and what is its role as a regulatory agency?", "a": "<p>The Federal Communications Commission regulates interstate and international radio, TV, wire, satellite and cable communications in the US. It has five commissioners, appointed by the President and confirmed by the Senate. Its aims include protecting consumers from unfair price rises or worse service, and preventing monopolies with barriers so high no one else can enter.</p>", "p": 180}, {"id": "ind-u1-49", "q": "What is net neutrality?", "a": "<p>The principle that internet service providers must treat all online data equally — no favouring, blocking or discriminating against particular users, sites or content. Without it, ISPs could restrict access (slowing downloads, uploads or certain apps) or deliberately speed up or slow down traffic depending on where it comes from or who owns it.</p><p class=\"muted\">US federal net neutrality rules have been repealed and reinstated several times; a 2025 court ruling struck down the FCC’s latest version.</p>", "p": 181}, {"id": "ind-u1-50", "q": "How did TikTok become such a popular app in an industry that's considered as being near a monopoly in terms of competition?", "a": "<p>TikTok (owned by ByteDance) sounds unremarkable — short videos, similar to Snapchat and Instagram/Facebook Stories. What set it apart was its focus on <b>user-generated content discovery</b>: almost anyone could go viral and get millions of views, unlike YouTube, whose front page is dominated by established (often sponsored) creators.</p><p>Vine, an early short-video app acquired by Twitter, was shut down — many blame an algorithm that over-promoted the same endorsed creators, making feeds repetitive. TikTok’s appeal is discovering new, unknown creators, so it’s less likely to make the same mistake.</p><p class=\"muted\">Since the book, TikTok’s US ownership has been a major regulatory fight — check the latest status before an interview.</p>", "p": 181}, {"id": "ind-u1-51", "q": "What major licensing deal did Spotify complete in 2020?", "a": "<p>In May 2020 Spotify signed an exclusive licensing deal, reported at about $100m, for <b>The Joe Rogan Experience</b>, one of the most popular US podcasts. CEO Daniel Ek saw podcasts and audiobooks — not just music — as Spotify’s future. It followed acquisitions of podcast companies Gimlet Media, Anchor and The Ringer, but JRE was the highest-profile move to make Spotify the leading audio platform.</p><p class=\"muted\">In 2024 Spotify renewed the deal on non-exclusive terms, with the show also on YouTube and other platforms.</p>", "p": 181}, {"id": "ind-u1-52", "q": "Tell me about an ongoing trend in the news industry.", "a": "<p>Writers and journalists going <b>independent</b> through paid email newsletters on platforms like Substack. With distrust of mainstream media high, writers get full control of their pricing and content, and are paid directly by subscribers rather than through ads — a decentralisation of news away from publishers.</p><p>Other trends: news moving further to mobile, aggregators (Reddit, Apple News), X/Twitter as a news source, and daily/weekly curation newsletters.</p>", "p": 181}]}], "ind-u2": [{"id": "ind-u2-l1", "title": "SaaS models, valuation & unit economics", "pages": "182–184", "cards": [{"id": "ind-u2-1", "q": "What are the advantages/disadvantages of being a vertical vs. horizontal software company?", "a": "<p><b>Horizontal software</b> serves many industries with broad solutions (Office 365, Salesforce CRM, QuickBooks).</p><ul><li><b>Pros:</b> a much larger TAM — a small slice of a huge market can beat leading a small one. Large markets take time to saturate, so the company has room to find its niche, refine strategy and narrow its target customers to the most profitable end markets.</li><li><b>Cons:</b> SaaS markets tend to be winner-takes-all, with a few products becoming the standard. Competition is cut-throat and involves big, well-funded (often public) rivals, so sales &amp; marketing spend is much higher — a race to reach customers.</li></ul><p><b>Vertical software</b> targets a specific niche industry.</p><ul><li><b>Pros:</b> a product that adds real value can quickly lead the niche, then build a tailored suite based on deep knowledge of customers’ needs — a durable moat that can reshape the industry. Lower churn (less threat from big players) and lower S&amp;M spend.</li><li><b>Cons:</b> higher risk — niches are often neglected for a reason (technical hurdles, specialisation, R&amp;D costs), and the market may not justify the spend. Products can take longer and cost more to build. And even a leader eventually runs out of room, forcing expansion into adjacent markets and geographies — at which point its S&amp;M advantage narrows.</li></ul>", "p": 182}, {"id": "ind-u2-2", "q": "Tell me about the trend of verticalization in the SaaS industry.", "a": "<p>Early on, investors gave vertical software less interest and lower valuations, assuming limited scalability ruled out multi-billion-dollar exits. Today almost every industry runs on software, largely thanks to <b>vertical solution providers (VSPs)</b>.</p><p>Their niches were slow to adopt technology and ignored by horizontal players because of the customisation and technical requirements involved — leaving lots of room for tailored, value-add products. The common pattern: land one product in an underserved niche, then build out a platform of tools for that customer base. With a head start and little competition, they can focus on product and deepen their advantage.</p><p>The result: far more VSPs, now valued at premiums, thanks to customisation, loyal low-churn customers and stronger pricing power.</p>", "p": 182}, {"id": "ind-u2-3", "q": "Which valuation multiples are used most commonly in SaaS?", "a": "<ul><li><b>EV/Revenue</b> remains the main multiple for public SaaS.</li><li><b>EV/EBITDA</b> is increasingly used as the sector matures and the market rewards healthy margins, especially where market leaders are clear.</li><li>For unprofitable companies: <b>EV/Revenue, EV/ARR</b>, and user multiples (EV/DAU, EV/MAU, EV/subscribers).</li><li>For acqui-hires (buying a company mainly for its team): <b>EV/total funding raised</b> and <b>EV/employee count</b>.</li></ul>", "p": 183}, {"id": "ind-u2-4", "q": "What is product-market fit?", "a": "<p>One of the biggest factors in whether a start-up succeeds. Marc Andreessen put it as “being in a good market with a product that can satisfy that market.”</p><p>It’s validated by consistent usage from existing users, organic customer growth and word of mouth. It’s qualitative and hard to measure, but you can see it in engagement, customer feedback and growth that accelerates as the product starts to “sell itself”.</p>", "p": 183}, {"id": "ind-u2-5", "q": "What does the LTV: CAC ratio represent and why is it an important metric to track?", "a": "<p>Arguably the most important software metric. <b>LTV</b> is the total value a customer generates over its lifetime; <b>CAC</b> is the cost of acquiring that customer.</p><div class=\"formula\">LTV:CAC = Customer lifetime value ÷ Customer acquisition cost</div><p>It answers: is the company getting enough value from customers to justify what it spends to win them? How much you spend to acquire a customer should depend on what that customer is worth.</p>", "p": 183}, {"id": "ind-u2-6", "q": "How do you calculate the customer lifetime value (LTV) and customer acquisition cost (CAC)?", "a": "<ol><li><b>Customer lifetime</b> (in months): <div class=\"formula\">Lifetime = 1 ÷ Monthly customer churn rate</div></li><li><b>ARPA</b> — keep the periods consistent (monthly): <div class=\"formula\">ARPA = MRR ÷ Number of accounts</div></li><li><b>LTV:</b> <div class=\"formula\">LTV = ARPA × Gross margin % ÷ MRR churn rate</div></li><li><b>CAC:</b> <div class=\"formula\">CAC = Total S&amp;M expense ÷ New customers acquired</div></li><li><b>LTV:CAC</b> = LTV ÷ CAC.</li></ol><p>E.g. ARPA $100, 80% gross margin, 2% monthly churn → LTV = $100 × 80% ÷ 2% = $4,000. With CAC of $1,000, LTV:CAC is 4.0x.</p>", "p": 184}, {"id": "ind-u2-7", "q": "What is the ideal LTV/CAC ratio that software companies target?", "a": "<ul><li><b>&gt;3.0x:</b> sustainable — the usual target for growing software companies.</li><li><b>&lt;1.0x:</b> unsustainable — the company struggles to monetise the customers it acquires.</li><li><b>&gt;5.0x</b> at an early-stage company: management may be <b>under</b>-investing and should spend more on acquiring customers.</li></ul>", "p": 184}, {"id": "ind-u2-8", "q": "Conceptually, what does customer lifetime value (LTV) represent?", "a": "<p>How much an average customer is worth to the company over the whole relationship. It helps predict future revenue per customer, and is compared with CAC to judge how cost-efficient acquisition is.</p>", "p": 184}, {"id": "ind-u2-9", "q": "How do you calculate customer churn?", "a": "<p>Churn (or attrition) is the rate at which customers cancel over a period: customers lost as a % of customers at the start.</p><div class=\"formula\">Churn rate = Customers churned ÷ Customers at start of period</div>", "p": 184}, {"id": "ind-u2-10", "q": "How would you calculate revenue churn?", "a": "<p><b>MRR churn:</b> MRR lost from churned customers as a % of starting MRR.</p><div class=\"formula\">MRR churn % = Churned MRR ÷ Starting MRR</div><p><b>Net MRR churn:</b> also counts expansion MRR from existing customers. If expansion outweighs losses, net churn turns <b>negative</b>.</p><div class=\"formula\">Net MRR churn % = (Churned MRR − Expansion MRR) ÷ Starting MRR</div>", "p": 184}]}, {"id": "ind-u2-l2", "title": "Bookings, billings & recurring revenue", "pages": "185–186", "cards": [{"id": "ind-u2-11", "q": "What are bookings and are they recognized as revenue under GAAP?", "a": "<p>Bookings are the value of contracts customers have committed to, usually annual or multi-year. They are <b>not</b> GAAP revenue yet, but they’re a key leading indicator for forecasting — showing the direction of growth and momentum.</p>", "p": 185}, {"id": "ind-u2-12", "q": "For software companies, why are bookings a better proxy than revenue to measure growth?", "a": "<p>Bookings are <b>forward-looking</b> and don’t understate the true value of customer contracts the way accrual revenue (recognised gradually over the contract) does. That makes them better for judging sales &amp; marketing performance, and a key metric for showing projected performance when raising VC money.</p>", "p": 185}, {"id": "ind-u2-13", "q": "Why would it be a mistake to use bookings and deferred revenue interchangeably?", "a": "<p>Both are unearned revenue, but:</p><ul><li><b>Bookings:</b> the customer has committed, but has neither <b>received</b> nor <b>paid</b> for the product yet.</li><li><b>Deferred revenue:</b> the customer has <b>already paid</b> upfront for services not yet delivered — it’s a balance sheet liability.</li></ul>", "p": 185}, {"id": "ind-u2-14", "q": "In SaaS, what are billings and how does it differ from bookings?", "a": "<p><b>Billings</b> are the invoices actually issued to customers for payment; <b>bookings</b> are the total contracted commitment.</p><p>E.g. a $12,000 annual contract billed quarterly: after the first quarter’s invoice, billings are <b>$3,000</b> and the remaining $9,000 of the booking is yet to be billed.</p><p class=\"muted\">The book says “first month” — with quarterly billing it’s the first quarter.</p>", "p": 185}, {"id": "ind-u2-15", "q": "An early-stage startup has a very low churn rate. Why might this be misleading?", "a": "<p>Early customers are usually <b>early adopters</b> — enthusiasts keen to try new technology, and the least likely to churn. They behave more like product testers than typical customers, so their low churn won’t hold once the company sells to the mainstream.</p>", "p": 185}, {"id": "ind-u2-16", "q": "In the SaaS industry, what is a reasonable customer churn rate?", "a": "<p>The book puts the norm at roughly <b>5% a year</b>, but it should always be benchmarked against close competitors with similar end customers.</p><p class=\"muted\">In practice, enterprise-focused SaaS tends to churn less than SMB-focused SaaS, so the right benchmark depends on the customer base.</p>", "p": 185}, {"id": "ind-u2-17", "q": "Why is product revenue preferred over service-based revenue in the software industry?", "a": "<p>Product (software/subscription) revenue has <b>higher gross margins</b> and scales far better. Services revenue (consulting, implementation) is less recurring, much lower margin and less scalable, since it needs more people to deliver.</p>", "p": 185}, {"id": "ind-u2-18", "q": "What are monthly recurring revenue and annual recurring revenue used to measure?", "a": "<p><b>MRR</b> is the predictable recurring revenue a subscription business earns each month. It includes subscription fees, upgrades, upselling and cross-selling, net of downgrades, discounts and churn. Each month’s MRR = last month’s MRR + net new MRR.</p><p><b>ARR</b> is simply MRR annualised:</p><div class=\"formula\">ARR = MRR × 12</div>", "p": 186}, {"id": "ind-u2-19", "q": "What does annual recurring revenue (ARR) mean in the SaaS context?", "a": "<p>The annualised value of a software company’s <b>recurring</b> revenue. Done properly, it <b>excludes</b> one-off fees — professional services, consulting, installation and set-up. It shows how much of total revenue is recurring.</p>", "p": 186}, {"id": "ind-u2-20", "q": "What are the flaws of the annual recurring revenue (ARR) metrics?", "a": "<p>ARR projects next year’s revenue from the latest month’s MRR. That implicitly assumes <b>no churn, no upselling and no downgrades</b> — and that the most recent month is the best guide to the future.</p>", "p": 186}]}, {"id": "ind-u2-l3", "title": "Growth & sales efficiency metrics", "pages": "186–188", "cards": [{"id": "ind-u2-21", "q": "How do you calculate net new MRR for a given period?", "a": "<div class=\"formula\">Net new MRR = New MRR + Expansion MRR − Churned MRR</div><p>New MRR comes from new customers, expansion MRR from existing customers spending more, and churned MRR is what’s lost from customers who leave.</p>", "p": 186}, {"id": "ind-u2-22", "q": "What are the different types of expansion MRR?", "a": "<ul><li><b>Upselling:</b> moving existing customers to a pricier, upgraded version with more features or capacity.</li><li><b>Cross-selling:</b> selling complementary products that enhance what they already use.</li><li><b>Add-ons:</b> letting customers unlock extra features or widgets not included in their plan.</li></ul>", "p": 186}, {"id": "ind-u2-23", "q": "Why is the net dollar retention an important metric to measure alongside the ARR?", "a": "<p>ARR alone can mislead: a company can grow ARR 100%+ a year while its <b>net dollar retention (NDR)</b> is poor (&lt;75%). Weak NDR will eventually drag ARR growth down if the underlying problems aren’t fixed, so it’s a core health check for early-stage SaaS.</p><div class=\"formula\">NDR = (Starting MRR + Expansion − Downgrades − Churn) ÷ Starting MRR × 100</div><ul><li><b>NDR &gt; 100%:</b> recurring revenue from existing customers is growing. Top SaaS companies beat this by a wide margin; most aim for ~100% or slightly above.</li><li><b>NDR &lt; 100%:</b> revenue from existing customers is shrinking due to downgrades and churn.</li></ul>", "p": 186}, {"id": "ind-u2-24", "q": "How are bookings accounted for when calculating MRR?", "a": "<p>A booking is the value of a long-term committed contract, so for MRR it’s spread (amortised) evenly over the contract’s months. E.g. a $36,000 three-year booking adds $1,000 of MRR.</p>", "p": 186}, {"id": "ind-u2-25", "q": "What are some ways that software companies can grow their recurring revenue?", "a": "<ul><li><b>New customers:</b> more S&amp;M spend, or entering new channels and markets.</li><li><b>Higher average selling prices:</b> needs pricing power.</li><li><b>More cross-selling:</b> it’s easier to sell complementary products to existing customers.</li><li><b>Higher upsell rate:</b> more revenue from existing customers.</li></ul>", "p": 187}, {"id": "ind-u2-26", "q": "What is CMRR, and how does it compare to MRR?", "a": "<p><b>Committed MRR</b> takes today’s MRR and layers on contracts already signed but not yet live, contracted upsells, contracted downgrades and the churn you expect:</p><div class=\"formula\">CMRR = MRR + New bookings + Upsell bookings − Downgrade bookings − Churn</div><p>Unlike MRR, it’s <b>forward-looking</b> and involves more judgement about what to include, so it’s more speculative — but arguably a better guide to future performance.</p>", "p": 187}, {"id": "ind-u2-27", "q": "In the SaaS industry, how would you measure sales efficiency?", "a": "<p>All sales efficiency metrics ask: for each dollar of S&amp;M spend, how much new recurring revenue did we generate?</p><p><b>Gross sales efficiency</b> (ignores churn):</p><div class=\"formula\">Gross sales efficiency = Gross new ARR ÷ S&amp;M expense</div><p><b>Net sales efficiency</b> (includes lost customers):</p><div class=\"formula\">Net new ARR = New ARR + Expansion ARR − Churned ARR<br>Net sales efficiency = Net new ARR (this quarter) ÷ S&amp;M spend (prior quarter)</div><p>Many public companies don’t disclose those inputs, so Scale Venture Partners created the <b>Magic Number</b>, using GAAP revenue instead:</p><div class=\"formula\">Magic Number = (Revenue this Q − Revenue last Q) × 4 ÷ S&amp;M spend last Q</div><p>&lt;0.75 is inefficient, 0.75–1.0 efficient, &gt;1.0 very efficient.</p>", "p": 187}, {"id": "ind-u2-28", "q": "What is the CAC Payback Period?", "a": "<p>The number of months it takes to earn back the cost of acquiring a customer. Shorter is better — the company breaks even on each customer sooner.</p><div class=\"formula\">CAC payback (months) = CAC ÷ (Average MRR per customer × Gross margin %)</div><p>The general aim is under 12 months. Best-in-class SaaS averages ~6–12 months; most are around 18.</p>", "p": 188}, {"id": "ind-u2-29", "q": "How do you calculate ARPA?", "a": "<p>Average revenue per account — revenue per active account, usually monthly (often used interchangeably with ARPU). A rising ARPA means each account is worth more as the business scales.</p><div class=\"formula\">ARPA = MRR ÷ Number of accounts</div><p>Use ARR instead of MRR for an annual figure.</p>", "p": 188}, {"id": "ind-u2-30", "q": "What is the difference between total contract value (TCV) and annual contract value (ACV)?", "a": "<p><b>TCV:</b> the full value of a contract, whatever its length (one month, six months, three years…).</p><p><b>ACV:</b> the contract’s value per 12 months. Because it’s annualised, it’s the better metric for comparing across companies and contracts.</p>", "p": 188}]}, {"id": "ind-u2-l4", "title": "Retention, TAM & SaaS analytics", "pages": "188–190", "cards": [{"id": "ind-u2-31", "q": "How can a software company decrease its churn?", "a": "<ul><li><b>Focused segmentation:</b> identify the lowest-churn customer segments and focus selling there. Through trial and error, learn where demand is strongest and double down on the most profitable segments — narrowing the target market is a key priority for any start-up.</li><li><b>Better customer engagement:</b> stay in contact with existing customers, offer extra insights and discounts, and always collect and act on feedback.</li><li><b>Upfront payments:</b> push annual plans with discounts or perks — customers on annual or long-term contracts are far less likely to churn.</li><li><b>Variable pricing:</b> offer options based on seats, licences or features. Some will downgrade, but more flexibility means fewer lost customers.</li></ul>", "p": 188}, {"id": "ind-u2-32", "q": "If a SaaS company has net negative churn, what does this mean?", "a": "<p>Expansion revenue from existing customers is <b>larger</b> than the revenue lost from cancellations and downgrades. New customers aren’t counted. So existing customers alone grow recurring revenue — even unusually high churn is more than offset.</p><p>It proves the product delivers real value: the company could weather a sharp drop in new sales. Still, negative feedback left unaddressed will eventually turn into churn.</p>", "p": 188}, {"id": "ind-u2-33", "q": "What is the importance of TAM in SaaS investing and how is it estimated?", "a": "<p>The total addressable market quantifies the revenue opportunity — and investors spend a lot of time on it. The most useful part is <b>segmenting</b> the market: which areas are growing or shrinking, customer categories, and spending patterns (cohort analysis).</p><p><b>Bottom-up</b> (most common): define the target customer, estimate how many there are, their growth and the likely penetration. Revenue = quantity × price, using data from past selling:</p><ul><li>B2C SaaS: Revenue = Users × ARPU</li><li>B2B SaaS: Revenue = Customers × ACV</li><li>Volume-based: Revenue = GMV or TPV × Take rate</li></ul><p><b>Top-down:</b> start from the total market size (from industry research) and apply an assumed market share. Quick and dirty rather than precise.</p>", "p": 189}, {"id": "ind-u2-34", "q": "What is the SaaS Quick Ratio?", "a": "<p>Popularised by Mamoon Hamid, it compares MRR gained to MRR lost over the same period:</p><div class=\"formula\">SaaS Quick Ratio = (New MRR + Expansion MRR) ÷ (Churned MRR + Contraction MRR)</div><ul><li><b>&lt;1:</b> churn is outpacing growth.</li><li><b>1–4:</b> growing, but losing a lot of potential to churn.</li><li><b>4+:</b> Hamid’s target.</li></ul><p>New MRR comes from new customers; expansion from upsells and cross-sells; churned MRR from cancellations; contraction from downgrades. Because SaaS markets tend to be winner-takes-all, market share and churn are critical.</p>", "p": 189}, {"id": "ind-u2-35", "q": "What is cohort analysis and its primary use case for SaaS companies?", "a": "<p>Grouping customers who share a characteristic (a cohort) and tracking their behaviour, to improve retention and LTV.</p><ul><li><b>Time-based:</b> grouped by when they signed up or purchased.</li><li><b>Segment-based:</b> by what they bought (product, plan, tier).</li><li><b>Size-based:</b> by customer size (SMB vs enterprise).</li></ul><p>Comparing cohorts reveals trends; the company then forms hypotheses and tests them. A key use is understanding <b>why</b> certain customers churn and what would reduce it.</p>", "p": 190}, {"id": "ind-u2-36", "q": "In SaaS, what is the purpose of A/B testing?", "a": "<p>Usually the hypothesis-testing step after cohort analysis. The company makes a change and tracks user behaviour, comparing two or more variants to see which performs better. It lets them improve the product gradually, choosing the best option based on real user data.</p>", "p": 190}, {"id": "ind-u2-37", "q": "What is the difference between gross margin and contribution margin?", "a": "<p><b>Gross margin:</b> revenue minus cost of goods sold — the direct costs (fixed and variable) of delivering the product.</p><div class=\"formula\">Gross profit = Revenue − COGS</div><p><b>Contribution margin:</b> profit per unit ignoring fixed costs — useful for comparing which product lines are most profitable per unit.</p><div class=\"formula\">Contribution margin = Revenue per unit − Variable cost per unit</div>", "p": 190}, {"id": "ind-u2-38", "q": "What is the \"Rule of 40\" in the SaaS industry?", "a": "<p>A company’s growth rate plus its profit margin should be <b>at least 40%</b>.</p><div class=\"formula\">Growth rate % + Profit margin % ≥ 40%</div><p>Growth is usually ARR or revenue growth; profit is usually EBITDA (or FCF) margin. So a company with low or negative profits can still earn a high multiple if growth offsets the burn — e.g. 60% growth with a −20% margin passes. Views differ on when it applies, but it’s a simple check that the growth-vs-spend trade-off is balanced, guarding against “growth at all costs”.</p>", "p": 190}]}], "ind-u3": [{"id": "ind-u3-l1", "title": "Bank financials & valuation", "pages": "191–193", "cards": [{"id": "ind-u3-1", "q": "Walk me through a bank’s income statement.", "a": "<ol><li><b>Net interest income (NII):</b> interest income minus interest expense — what the bank earns on loans versus what it pays depositors who fund them.</li><li><b>Provision for credit losses:</b> an accrual for expected losses on bad loans. NII minus provisions (“NII after provisions”) is the bank’s core operating profitability.</li><li><b>Non-interest income:</b> fees, commissions, service charges and trading gains.</li><li><b>Non-interest expense:</b> mainly salaries and benefits, plus occupancy and equipment, technology, amortisation, marketing and insurance.</li><li><b>Taxes</b> → <b>net income</b>.</li></ol>", "p": 191}, {"id": "ind-u3-2", "q": "Walk me through a bank’s balance sheet.", "a": "<p><b>Assets:</b> the biggest is the <b>loan portfolio</b> — residential and commercial real estate plus other consumer and commercial loans — followed by investment securities and cash.</p><p><b>Liabilities:</b> mostly <b>deposits</b>, the main source of funding. Interest-bearing deposits create interest expense; non-interest-bearing ones don’t. The rest is short- and long-term borrowings.</p><p><b>Equity:</b> as for any company — common stock, treasury stock, preferred stock, retained earnings and other comprehensive income.</p>", "p": 191}, {"id": "ind-u3-3", "q": "How are the financials of a bank different from a traditional company?", "a": "<p>For a normal company, revenue, COGS and SG&amp;A make up operating income, with interest, other gains/losses and tax below it as non-operating items. For a bank, that split doesn’t work: <b>interest income is its core revenue</b> and <b>interest expense is its main operating cost</b>. A bank’s P&amp;L is driven directly by the size and yield of its assets (loans) and the cost of its liabilities (deposits).</p>", "p": 191}, {"id": "ind-u3-4", "q": "What is the impact of an inverted yield curve on a bank’s profits?", "a": "<p>Banks <b>borrow short</b> (deposits, interbank loans) and <b>lend long</b> (mortgages, car loans, business loans). The wider the gap between long- and short-term yields, the more profitable they are. When the curve <b>flattens or inverts</b>, long yields fall relative to short ones and bank profits are squeezed. The size of the hit depends on each bank’s loan and investment mix (its duration exposure), but as a rule, profitability falls as the curve flattens or inverts.</p>", "p": 191}, {"id": "ind-u3-5", "q": "How do you value a commercial bank?", "a": "<p>The common approaches are all <b>levered</b> — they value equity directly:</p><ul><li>Levered DCF</li><li>Dividend discount model (DDM)</li><li>Residual income model</li><li>Comps using equity multiples (P/B, P/E)</li></ul><p>You can’t separate a bank’s operating value from its non-operating value, because its core operations <i>are</i> earning interest net of interest expense. So comps must use equity multiples like P/B and P/E, not EV/EBITDA, EV/Revenue or EV/EBIT.</p>", "p": 192}, {"id": "ind-u3-6", "q": "Walk me through valuing a bank using a levered DCF.", "a": "<p>An unlevered DCF won’t work because a bank’s operating and financing cash flows can’t be separated. Instead:</p><ol><li>Forecast <b>levered free cash flows</b> for 5–10 years.</li><li>Calculate a terminal value (perpetuity growth or exit multiple).</li><li>Discount both at the <b>cost of equity</b>.</li><li>The sum of the present values is the bank’s <b>equity value</b>.</li></ol>", "p": 192}, {"id": "ind-u3-7", "q": "Walk me through a bank valuation using the dividend discount model (DDM).", "a": "<p>Banks usually pay out large dividends, so a DDM is common: forecast future dividends and discount them at the cost of equity to get equity value. A <b>3-stage DDM</b> is typical:</p><ol><li><b>Development stage (3–5 years):</b> forecast dividends explicitly and discount them.</li><li><b>Transition stage:</b> instead of explicit forecasts, derive dividends assuming ROE gradually converges towards the cost of equity — a mature bank can’t earn far above its cost of equity forever.</li><li><b>Maturity stage:</b> the present value of all later dividends, using a perpetual growth rate or a terminal P/B multiple.</li></ol>", "p": 192}, {"id": "ind-u3-8", "q": "Walk me through a bank valuation using the residual income model. Why is it arguably better than the DCF or DDM?", "a": "<p>It reduces the DDM’s heavy reliance on terminal value.</p><div class=\"formula\">Equity value = Book value of equity + PV of future residual income</div><p>Residual income is net income above what shareholders require. E.g. a bank with $1bn of book equity and a 10% cost of equity that earns $150m next year has residual income of <b>$50m</b> ($150m − $1bn × 10%). Those excess returns are added to book value.</p><p>By the terminal period, excess returns are assumed to fade to zero, so the terminal value is <b>zero</b>. That avoids the DCF/DDM problem of a terminal value that mixes value creation with simply preserving value.</p>", "p": 192}, {"id": "ind-u3-9", "q": "What multiples are appropriate for valuing a bank?", "a": "<ul><li><b>P/B:</b> price per share ÷ book value per share.</li><li><b>P/E:</b> price per share ÷ EPS.</li><li><b>P/TBV:</b> price per share ÷ tangible book value per share.</li></ul>", "p": 193}, {"id": "ind-u3-10", "q": "Why is the unlevered DCF approach inappropriate for banks?", "a": "<p>An unlevered DCF looks at cash flows <b>before</b> the effects of debt. For a bank, interest income and interest expense effectively <i>are</i> its revenue and cost of goods sold, so stripping them out makes no sense. Separating operating earnings from interest is useful for normal companies but pointless for banks.</p>", "p": 193}]}, {"id": "ind-u3-l2", "title": "IBs, asset managers & FinTech basics", "pages": "193–195", "cards": [{"id": "ind-u3-11", "q": "How would you forecast the revenue of a boutique investment bank?", "a": "<p>A boutique earns mainly <b>advisory fees</b> on M&amp;A and on raising debt or equity. Since banking is relationship-driven and deal flow depends on senior bankers bringing in and closing deals, one approach uses <b>revenue per employee</b> as the driver, with assumptions for revenue per head and headcount growth.</p><p>Sanity check: back out the implied number of deals from forecast revenue, an average deal size and an average fee %, and compare it with historical deal flow.</p><p>Also consider the industry/product focus (which affects deal count, growth and cyclicality) and the firm’s trajectory, ranking, speciality and people. That’s why a large bank buying a boutique is really paying for its senior bankers’ relationships, reputation and expected deal flow — and why <b>earn-outs</b> are used to keep senior partners from leaving and to align incentives.</p>", "p": 193}, {"id": "ind-u3-12", "q": "How does a passively managed fund generate revenue?", "a": "<p>Revenue is driven by <b>AUM</b>. The firm charges a <b>management fee</b> as a % of AUM to cover running the portfolio and operating costs; the more active and specialised the strategy, the higher the %.</p><p>For mutual funds this shows up in the <b>management expense ratio (MER)</b>: the fund’s management fee, operating expenses and taxes for the year divided by average AUM — expressed as a % of the money invested.</p>", "p": 193}, {"id": "ind-u3-13", "q": "How would you value an actively managed alternative investment management firm?", "a": "<p>Alternative managers (hedge funds, PE) earn two kinds of cash flow with very different risk profiles, so use a <b>sum-of-the-parts</b>:</p><ul><li><b>Management fees</b> (~1–2% of capital): stable and easier to model. Apply a market multiple to current or next year’s fee earnings.</li><li><b>Performance fees / carried interest</b> (~15–20% of returns above a hurdle): earned when investments are exited, so lumpy and hard to forecast. Apply a <b>much lower</b> multiple.</li></ul>", "p": 194}, {"id": "ind-u3-14", "q": "What types of multiples are used to value asset management firms?", "a": "<p>Mainly <b>P/E</b> and <b>EV/EBITDA</b>. <b>EV/AUM</b> is sometimes used, but only works for very similar peers — you can’t compare a passive index manager with an active equity fund on EV/AUM, because their fees as a % of AUM and expected returns differ so much.</p>", "p": 194}, {"id": "ind-u3-15", "q": "What is securities lending?", "a": "<p>A lender temporarily lends stocks, bonds or other securities to a borrower (often to cover short sales). The borrower posts <b>collateral</b> and pays a <b>borrowing fee</b>, and must return the securities on request or on the agreed date.</p>", "p": 194}, {"id": "ind-u3-16", "q": "What are the three main types of securities lending models?", "a": "<ol><li><b>In-house:</b> large investors with the scale to run their own lending desk (e.g. Vanguard, BlackRock).</li><li><b>Custodian lending:</b> custodians bundle custody, fund administration and securities lending, pooling assets from many clients.</li><li><b>Third-party agents:</b> specialist lenders or prime-brokerage-linked firms that lend assets not held at the client’s custodian.</li></ol>", "p": 194}, {"id": "ind-u3-17", "q": "What are the two fee models in securities lending?", "a": "<p><b>Discretionary (agency) lending:</b> the traditional model. The lending agent negotiates each loan with borrowers on a best-efforts basis, based on fluctuating daily demand. The lender earns the market rate and pays the agent a fee.</p><p><b>Exclusive arrangement:</b> the lender negotiates exclusive access to its portfolio (or part of it) with one principal borrower for a <b>flat fee</b>. Steadier income, but less upside — though deals can include profit-sharing above a threshold.</p>", "p": 194}, {"id": "ind-u3-18", "q": "What is FinTech?", "a": "<p>Financial technology: using technology to deliver financial services faster and more efficiently — from mobile banking and investing through apps to sending money to friends online.</p>", "p": 195}, {"id": "ind-u3-19", "q": "What multiples would you use to value a FinTech company?", "a": "<p>Most common: <b>EV/Revenue, EV/EBITDA and P/E</b>. For high-growth sectors, LTM multiples are often meaningless, so use 1- and 2-year forward multiples. Outside the leaders, <b>EV/Revenue</b> dominates, since many FinTechs are unprofitable or barely profitable.</p><p>Industry-specific multiples:</p><ul><li><b>Digital banking &amp; payments:</b> EV/total payment volume (TPV), EV/P2P transfers, EV/premium users.</li><li><b>Transfer services &amp; marketplaces:</b> EV/gross transaction volume (GTV), EV/origination volume, EV/revenue per transaction.</li><li><b>Brokerages:</b> EV/daily average revenue trades (DARTs), EV/premium subscriptions.</li></ul>", "p": 195}, {"id": "ind-u3-20", "q": "How do most FinTech start-ups generate revenue?", "a": "<p>Mostly a <b>brokerage-style, fee-based</b> model — a small % of each transaction. That covers digital banking, payments, money transfer, stock brokerages and lending marketplaces (e.g. a P2P transfer platform charging 2–4% on international transfers).</p><p>Other models: flat <b>subscription</b> fees (e.g. robo-advisors) and <b>interest income</b> for direct lenders that lend off their own balance sheet.</p>", "p": 195}]}, {"id": "ind-u3-l3", "title": "Challenger banks & digital banking", "pages": "195–197", "cards": [{"id": "ind-u3-21", "q": "Tell me about the trend of challenger banks.", "a": "<p>Branchless digital banks that have won millions of customers, with many reaching unicorn valuations. They “challenge” incumbent banks, which consumers criticise for slow processing, hidden fees and lack of transparency. Leaders include <b>Chime, N26, Current, Revolut and Varo</b>.</p>", "p": 195}, {"id": "ind-u3-22", "q": "From the consumer perspective, what is the benefit of a challenger bank over a traditional bank?", "a": "<ul><li>Strong mobile apps and online presence that appeal to digital natives.</li><li>Quick account opening — often minutes.</li><li>Transparent fees with no hidden charges, and cheaper premium features.</li><li>A better overall digital experience.</li><li>Perks like Chime’s early paycheck access, skipping the usual 2–3 day float.</li></ul>", "p": 195}, {"id": "ind-u3-23", "q": "Are challenger banks actually “banks” in the traditional sense?", "a": "<p>Often not at first. Many launch without a banking licence and operate through a <b>partner bank</b>. As they grow, they either keep relying on those partners or apply for their own licence.</p>", "p": 195}, {"id": "ind-u3-24", "q": "What is the difference between a neo bank and a challenger bank?", "a": "<p>The terms are often used interchangeably, but strictly:</p><ul><li><b>Neo banks:</b> fully digital with no physical presence, serving customers only through apps and websites, and <b>without a full banking licence</b> — they rely on a partner bank.</li><li><b>Challenger banks:</b> hold a <b>full banking licence</b>, may have a small physical presence, and compete directly with incumbents.</li></ul>", "p": 196}, {"id": "ind-u3-25", "q": "Given their current business model, do challenger banks have a pathway to becoming profitable someday?", "a": "<p>Possibly — via <b>freemium</b>. Most offer a free basic account but increasingly push premium tiers: N26 has a basic account and a premium “Black” tier with travel, phone and insurance perks; Revolut has a free account plus paid subscription plans.</p><p>Many are still burning cash with a single-minded focus on growth, but freemium is the best way to keep acquiring customers while raising money from investors — and convert some into paying users.</p><p class=\"muted\">Since the book, several (e.g. Revolut, Nubank, Monzo) have become profitable.</p>", "p": 196}, {"id": "ind-u3-26", "q": "Why might consumer FinTech start-ups appear to be struggling to monetize their user base?", "a": "<p>Their focus has been on <b>user growth</b>, not revenue or profit, and it’s unclear how they’ll monetise. Some leaders, like Monzo and Revolut, reported deepening losses, layoffs and pay cuts, and complaints about worse service.</p><p>The core problem: users (mostly young) signed up because the apps were <b>free</b>, quick to join and easy to use, offering control and convenience. Charging them is hard. Many challenger banks expected <b>interchange income</b> from debit card use to make them profitable, but that’s proven difficult — not least because many customers use them as a side app, not their main bank.</p>", "p": 196}, {"id": "ind-u3-27", "q": "Tell me about the current state of the banking services start-up scene.", "a": "<p>Consumer digital banking — one of FinTech’s best-funded areas — became overcrowded, and monetisation worries grew. Start-ups began restricting free tiers, charging above usage limits and pushing premium plans; some even added overdraft, card replacement and withdrawal fees, contradicting their consumer-friendly pitch.</p><p>Meanwhile incumbents adapted, and Big Tech moved in — Google announced Plex, a fee-free account in Google Pay with 11 partner banks. The book expected 2021 to be make-or-break, with failures (e.g. Monedo’s insolvency) and more mergers, partnerships and exits to larger companies.</p><p class=\"muted\">Google cancelled Plex in 2021.</p>", "p": 196}, {"id": "ind-u3-28", "q": "How have traditional financial institutions responded to the new trends of digital banking?", "a": "<p>The challengers exposed real problems, and incumbents initially looked under threat. But big banks responded by launching much better apps, which slowed the momentum of consumer FinTech. Early-2020 rate cuts also nudged many customers back to their original banks.</p><p>Pressured by new entrants and the pandemic, almost every large bank invested in its digital infrastructure — better mobile apps, instant digital customer service and chatbots, and more transparency. COVID turned out to be a useful catalyst for traditional banks.</p>", "p": 197}, {"id": "ind-u3-29", "q": "Tell me about the controversy surrounding Robinhood and payment for order flow.", "a": "<p>In December 2020 the SEC charged Robinhood with misleading customers by not disclosing that it was paid by high-frequency trading firms to route customer orders to them — and with failing to get customers the best execution.</p><p>Investors thought Robinhood earned mainly from Gold memberships, interest on cash and securities lending. In fact, more than half its revenue came from <b>payment for order flow</b>: market makers like Citadel Securities pay brokers for the right to execute their customers’ trades.</p><p>PFOF isn’t illegal and is widespread, but the lack of transparency and how much Robinhood depended on it raised a bigger question: if a leading FinTech unicorn near IPO struggles to earn from its own products, can other FinTechs?</p><p class=\"muted\">Robinhood paid $65m to settle and went public in July 2021.</p>", "p": 197}, {"id": "ind-u3-30", "q": "Why did N26 exit the UK market in early 2020?", "a": "<p><b>Brexit.</b> EU FinTechs use “passporting”: get a banking licence in one EU state, then operate across all of them. N26 held a German (EU) licence, which stopped covering the UK after Brexit, so it shut its UK operations.</p>", "p": 197}]}, {"id": "ind-u3-l4", "title": "Open banking, payments & lending", "pages": "198–200", "cards": [{"id": "ind-u3-31", "q": "What does open banking mean?", "a": "<p>Letting <b>third parties</b>, with the customer’s permission, access that customer’s banking and financial account data. Since integration is a central FinTech theme, open banking has created huge opportunities for start-ups to improve the banking experience — it’s a leading driver of innovation in traditional banking.</p>", "p": 198}, {"id": "ind-u3-32", "q": "What role do APIs play in open banking?", "a": "<p>APIs make <b>Banking-as-a-Service (BaaS)</b> possible — the end-to-end connection between banks and third-party start-ups. They let FinTechs build new layers of services on top of a bank’s core products while keeping customers’ financial data secure.</p>", "p": 198}, {"id": "ind-u3-33", "q": "Does open banking harm traditional financial institutions?", "a": "<p>No — the opposite. Third-party integrations had been delayed by regulation for years, but now that open banking has taken off, a bank that refuses risks losing customers to more modern providers. Integrations complement banking services and bring new growth and revenue, so banks have an incentive to race to offer as many as possible.</p>", "p": 198}, {"id": "ind-u3-34", "q": "What is the state of the payments industry landscape today, and how has it been changing?", "a": "<p>Businesses and consumers are moving from cash to digital, and contactless payments (e.g. NFC mobile payments) look set to become the norm online and in store. Tech giants have moved in — Apple Pay, Samsung Pay, Amazon Pay, Google Pay. With rising demand for security (biometrics, sensor and chip technology), payments looks like one of FinTech’s most competitive areas, led by incumbents like Square (now Block) and PayPal plus those new entrants.</p>", "p": 198}, {"id": "ind-u3-35", "q": "What impact do you predict COVID-19 to have on the usage of QR codes?", "a": "<p>QR codes are decades old but hadn’t taken off for US payments. COVID created demand for no-touch payments and showed how reliable QR codes are. Many merchants started accepting QR payments (some stopped taking cash), and apps like Uber Eats promoted contactless ordering — a tailwind for wider adoption.</p>", "p": 198}, {"id": "ind-u3-36", "q": "What does peer-to-peer (P2P) lending involve?", "a": "<p>Also called “social lending”: borrowing directly from other individuals without a traditional bank in the middle. A P2P platform usually matches lenders and borrowers automatically; being online with low overhead, costs are much lower.</p><p>Note that most P2P <b>transaction volume</b> is actually money transfers through apps like Venmo, Cash App and Apple Pay, linked to users’ bank accounts.</p>", "p": 198}, {"id": "ind-u3-37", "q": "Can you tell me about some of the lending models that have emerged over the past few years?", "a": "<ul><li><b>P2P lending:</b> platforms match individual or small-business borrowers with investors, setting rates and terms using data-driven risk assessment.</li><li><b>Point-of-sale lending:</b> merchants offer financing (instalments) at checkout, online or in store.</li><li><b>FinTech–bank partnerships:</b> data aggregators, marketplaces and niche start-ups (e.g. SMB-focused) partner with banks, which provide the financing while the FinTech’s transaction data improves credit analysis.</li><li><b>Supply-chain finance:</b> non-bank platforms offering working capital solutions to underserved SMBs (e.g. dynamic discounting, reverse factoring).</li></ul>", "p": 199}, {"id": "ind-u3-38", "q": "What is point-of-sale lending?", "a": "<p>Also called <b>buy now, pay later (BNPL)</b>: consumers split a purchase into scheduled instalments at checkout, typically for little or no fee. Adoption has grown fast in the US, especially among millennials who like the flexibility. Businesses are also moving to cloud-based POS systems, which should speed up checkout. Leaders include <b>Affirm</b> (co-founded by Max Levchin), <b>Klarna</b> and <b>Afterpay</b> (now owned by Block).</p>", "p": 199}, {"id": "ind-u3-39", "q": "Why have lending companies such as Square relied less on the FICO score in recent years when assessing a borrower?", "a": "<p>Digital lenders like Square and Stripe own a lot of <b>transaction data</b>. Instead of the traditional FICO score, they use algorithms on the merchant’s payment history on their platform — total payment volume, share of repeat customers, payment frequency — to judge credit health. Relying on data rather than long applications and background checks lets them lend faster and make their own lending decisions.</p>", "p": 199}, {"id": "ind-u3-40", "q": "What are real-time payment (RTP) systems?", "a": "<p>In 2017 The Clearing House launched the US <b>RTP network</b>, allowing payments to clear and settle instantly, instead of the 1–2 days of traditional ACH. Helped by mobile adoption, real-time payments let consumers, merchants and banks move money much faster, setting a new standard worldwide.</p><p class=\"muted\">The Fed launched its own instant payment service, FedNow, in 2023.</p>", "p": 200}]}, {"id": "ind-u3-l5", "title": "Payments players & FinTech niches", "pages": "200–202", "cards": [{"id": "ind-u3-41", "q": "What role does Stripe play in the payments industry?", "a": "<p>Founded in 2010 in San Francisco to build “economic infrastructure for the internet”, Stripe is one of the most influential FinTechs globally. It serves millions of businesses — start-ups, e-commerce stores and Fortune 500 companies including Amazon. Through its software and APIs, customers can accept online payments and transfers, while Stripe handles the technical implementation, fraud prevention and banking infrastructure.</p>", "p": 200}, {"id": "ind-u3-42", "q": "What did Square accomplish in 2020 that was an important milestone for the FinTech industry?", "a": "<p>The FDIC conditionally approved Square for an <b>industrial loan company (ILC)</b> charter, letting it operate a bank — Square Financial Services in Utah, which launched in 2021 to offer small-business loans to its merchants and deposit products. Square had pursued the charter for about three years against opposition from bank lobbyists and regulators; it was the first new ILC approval in over a decade.</p>", "p": 200}, {"id": "ind-u3-43", "q": "What led to the downfall of Lending Club, a former FinTech unicorn?", "a": "<p>Lending Club pioneered <b>P2P lending</b> — individuals lending to each other via its platform — and was valued at over $9bn at its 2014 IPO. Then came a loan scandal involving its CEO, and the discovery that its credit-risk algorithm wasn’t working as intended. Its value fell below ~$350m.</p><p>It eventually bought Radius Bank, shut its P2P platform and became a bank focused on institutional investors. The assumption that people would repay more reliably when the lender was another individual turned out to be wrong — and assessing individuals’ credit risk proved hard.</p>", "p": 200}, {"id": "ind-u3-44", "q": "What is a payday loan, and how does the process work?", "a": "<p>A controversial, very short-term loan (usually a couple of weeks) for emergencies, traditionally from storefront lenders, though increasingly online. Applications take minutes and the money goes into the borrower’s bank account. The lender requires a <b>post-dated cheque</b> covering the principal plus very high interest, guaranteeing repayment on the due date. Borrowers in a tight spot accept because their credit history isn’t rejected — often not even checked.</p>", "p": 200}, {"id": "ind-u3-45", "q": "What is InsurTech?", "a": "<p>Technology that makes the traditionally slow, inefficient insurance model more efficient. Examples:</p><ul><li>Chatbots and apps that streamline back-end processes and reduce customer service headcount.</li><li>Risk modelling to predict losses, and demand modelling to set premiums.</li><li>Fraud detection by spotting abnormal patterns.</li><li>Automated claims processing and ML-driven underwriting.</li></ul><p>The result is digital premiums and claims and higher margins. Leaders included Oscar Health, Root, Bright Health, Clover Health, Lemonade and Policygenius.</p>", "p": 201}, {"id": "ind-u3-46", "q": "What is crowdfunding and what are some of its benefits?", "a": "<p>Raising small amounts of money from many people, usually through online platforms, often in exchange for a stake in a small private venture. It uses the internet’s reach to tap a huge pool of potential investors, and benefits society by encouraging entrepreneurship.</p>", "p": 201}, {"id": "ind-u3-47", "q": "What does card-not-present fraud mean?", "a": "<p>Fraud where the card isn’t physically presented to the merchant — online or phone transactions. It’s growing as scams get more sophisticated and more payments go digital.</p>", "p": 201}, {"id": "ind-u3-48", "q": "What are Robo-Advisors?", "a": "<p>Platforms that automatically manage and rebalance a portfolio using algorithms, based on the user’s goals — instead of a human financial advisor. Betterment was one of the first to go mainstream.</p><p><b>For firms:</b> less need for human advisors and labour costs, so banks invest heavily in AI advisors and chatbots, which are becoming smarter and more accepted.</p><p><b>For customers:</b> lower fees and low minimum investments. Robo-advisors mostly automate <b>passive</b> strategies (ETFs, index funds) for long-term investing, so they carry less risk.</p>", "p": 201}, {"id": "ind-u3-49", "q": "What does the “underbanked” refer to?", "a": "<p>People without proper access to banking — no account, or no bank branches or formal financial services nearby. Giving them access, especially through location-independent digital banks, is seen as one of FinTech’s most important purposes.</p><p class=\"muted\">Strictly, “unbanked” means no account at all; “underbanked” means having one but relying on alternatives like payday loans or cheque cashers.</p>", "p": 202}]}, {"id": "ind-u3-l6", "title": "Crypto, brokerage & compliance", "pages": "202–203", "cards": [{"id": "ind-u3-50", "q": "What are cryptocurrencies?", "a": "<p>Digital assets secured by cryptography and run on a <b>decentralised</b> system, so no central authority controls people’s wallets. They rely on blockchain ledgers and smart contracts.</p>", "p": 202}, {"id": "ind-u3-51", "q": "How would you value Bitcoin?", "a": "<p>Bitcoin produces no cash flows, so it can’t be valued like a traditional asset. Its price reflects beliefs about how likely it is to become an established store of value or medium of exchange.</p><p>Two views from the book:</p><ul><li><b>Warren Buffett:</b> cryptocurrencies have no value because they produce nothing.</li><li><b>Chamath Palihapitiya</b> (Social Capital): it has value as a hedge against the traditional financial system, being uncorrelated with other markets. Like gold, it’s a non-productive asset worth what others will pay — and could replace gold as a safe haven. He suggested investors hold at least ~1% in Bitcoin, not that it should be a large part of a portfolio.</li></ul><p class=\"muted\">Since the book: big price swings, the FTX collapse (2022) and US spot Bitcoin ETFs (approved January 2024).</p>", "p": 202}, {"id": "ind-u3-52", "q": "What is an ICO?", "a": "<p>An <b>initial coin offering</b> — the crypto equivalent of an IPO. The key difference: IPO investors get equity, while ICO investors get <b>digital tokens</b>. ICOs were largely unregulated and prone to exit scams, which drew regulatory scrutiny and hurt crypto’s public image.</p>", "p": 202}, {"id": "ind-u3-53", "q": "What is the function of blockchain technology?", "a": "<p>A secure digital ledger recording every transaction. Each <b>block</b> holds transaction data (date, time, amount) and is linked to the previous block in a <b>chain</b>, designed to be practically impossible to alter. It underpins cryptocurrencies, most notably Bitcoin.</p>", "p": 202}, {"id": "ind-u3-54", "q": "What does the trend toward “DeFi” signify?", "a": "<p><b>Decentralised finance</b>: financial applications built on crypto and blockchain. It implies disrupting traditional intermediaries and removing central authorities, giving users more control since the middleman is cut out. E.g. lending platforms run by <b>smart contracts</b> rather than banks.</p>", "p": 202}, {"id": "ind-u3-55", "q": "What impact did Robinhood’s zero-commission pricing have on the brokerage industry?", "a": "<p>It forced incumbents to follow. Charles Schwab, TD Ameritrade and E*Trade cut or eliminated commissions on many trades to avoid losing customers. The disruption is widely credited with prompting Schwab’s acquisition of TD Ameritrade (completed 2020).</p>", "p": 203}, {"id": "ind-u3-56", "q": "What is the purpose of a FinTech sandbox?", "a": "<p>A controlled testing environment that mimics real-world use, with safeguards so failure has no serious consequences. FinTechs can pilot new products there before getting licensed and launching to market, where the stakes are much higher.</p>", "p": 203}, {"id": "ind-u3-57", "q": "What does Anti-Money Laundering (AML) refer to?", "a": "<p>Rules designed to stop criminals passing off illegally obtained money as legitimate income. AML laws require financial institutions to monitor clients and report suspicious activity.</p>", "p": 203}, {"id": "ind-u3-58", "q": "What is RegTech?", "a": "<p>Using software and technology to manage regulatory processes in financial services — mainly regulatory monitoring, reporting and compliance checks. It helps firms comply, and helps regulators monitor and enforce rules.</p>", "p": 203}, {"id": "ind-u3-59", "q": "What does “KYC” refer to in FinTech?", "a": "<p><b>Know Your Customer:</b> the process of verifying a client’s identity through identification and background checks. It prevents identity fraud, and is usually a legal requirement for financial institutions, closely tied to AML.</p>", "p": 203}]}], "ind-u4": [{"id": "ind-u4-l1", "title": "Healthcare landscape & the drug supply chain", "pages": "204–206", "cards": [{"id": "ind-u4-1", "q": "Provide a broad overview of how the healthcare industry is segmented.", "a": "<ul><li><b>Pharma:</b> biotech, large pharma, specialty pharma, generics.</li><li><b>Services:</b> contract research organisations (CROs), pharmacy benefit managers (PBMs), pharmacies, distributors, healthcare IT (HCIT).</li><li><b>Payers:</b> managed care organisations (health insurers) and the Centers for Medicare &amp; Medicaid Services (CMS).</li><li><b>Providers:</b> hospitals, urgent care, inpatient rehab, ambulatory surgery centres, long-term care hospitals, outpatient rehab, skilled nursing, home health and hospice.</li><li><b>Med-tech:</b> medical devices and equipment.</li></ul>", "p": 204}, {"id": "ind-u4-2", "q": "What are some ongoing industry trends in the healthcare sector?", "a": "<ul><li><b>Vertical integration:</b> payers buying or building their own PBMs (Cigna–Express Scripts; CVS, which owns PBM Caremark, buying insurer Aetna); retail pharmacies aligning with distributors; pharmacies partnering with or acquiring payers.</li><li><b>Integrated delivery networks (IDNs):</b> providers and health plans in a region combining into one ecosystem under a single brand, aligning the sub-sectors.</li><li><b>Value-based care:</b> concern that care is paid for by quantity rather than quality is driving a shift to <b>outcome-based</b> reimbursement.</li><li><b>Consumerisation:</b> urgent care centres, more patient access to personal health data, telemedicine.</li></ul><p>You don’t need a science background for healthcare banking interviews — a basic grasp of the business models and a few current trends is enough.</p>", "p": 204}, {"id": "ind-u4-3", "q": "What are the main differences between biotech and other types of pharma companies?", "a": "<p><b>Biotech</b> makes drugs from <b>living organisms</b> (biologics); other pharma mostly uses <b>chemical compounds</b>. Biotechs are typically high-growth and built around one product or therapeutic area. Large pharma (Pfizer, Merck) is far more diversified, with many product lines across several therapeutic areas.</p>", "p": 205}, {"id": "ind-u4-4", "q": "What do inpatient and outpatient refer to when discussing providers?", "a": "<p><b>Inpatient:</b> the patient is admitted and stays overnight in a hospital or similar facility.</p><p><b>Outpatient:</b> the patient is treated but doesn’t stay overnight.</p>", "p": 205}, {"id": "ind-u4-5", "q": "Tell me the difference between acute and post-acute care.", "a": "<p><b>Acute care:</b> hospital treatment for short-term, often severe injuries and emergencies — urgent care, ICUs, emergency rooms.</p><p><b>Post-acute care:</b> treatment after an acute hospital stay, at non-hospital facilities — from short-term rehab to longer-term restorative care (physical therapy, skilled nursing, rehab facilities, psychiatric care).</p><p>A typical care pathway: primary care physician → hospital (acute) → rehab or skilled nursing facility (post-acute) → physical therapy (post-acute).</p>", "p": 205}, {"id": "ind-u4-6", "q": "What are the two channels for pharmaceutical drug distribution?", "a": "<p><b>Retail channel</b></p><ul><li><b>Product:</b> branded or generic manufacturer → distributor (often aligned with a pharmacy chain) → retail pharmacy → patient.</li><li><b>Money:</b> the patient’s co-pay passes through the pharmacy to the PBM. The payer pays the PBM based on negotiated prices for drugs on its <b>formulary</b> (list of covered drugs). The PBM pays pharmacies, and pharmacies pay manufacturers, all at negotiated prices.</li></ul><p>All this negotiation lets PBMs and pharmacies earn a <b>spread</b> between prices — which is why payers and PBMs, and pharmacies and payers, have been merging: to capture or squeeze out that “excess” profit.</p><p><b>Mail-order channel:</b> product goes straight from the manufacturer to a (usually PBM-run) mail-order pharmacy, then to the patient. Co-pays go directly to the PBM, the payer pays the PBM, and the PBM pays manufacturers.</p>", "p": 205}, {"id": "ind-u4-7", "q": "What is the biggest cost driver for pharma companies?", "a": "<ol><li><b>R&amp;D</b> — mainly clinical trials, by far the biggest cost. Getting through the trial stages and regulatory approval typically takes 5–7 years.</li><li><b>Manufacturing</b> — producing active pharmaceutical ingredients (APIs).</li><li><b>Sales &amp; marketing</b> to doctors and patients — a distant third.</li></ol>", "p": 206}, {"id": "ind-u4-8", "q": "What roles do pharmacy benefit managers (PBMs) play in the industry?", "a": "<p>PBMs manage prescription drug spending for insurers and try to keep drug costs low. They negotiate prices with drug makers and pharmacies, decide which drugs (brand or generic) get dispensed once a prescription reaches the pharmacy, and push generic use and mail-order.</p><p>They earn the <b>spread</b> between what payers pay them and what they pay pharmacies and manufacturers — which gives them an incentive to drive drug prices down.</p>", "p": 206}, {"id": "ind-u4-9", "q": "Explain the purpose of rebates in the context of pharmaceutical pricing.", "a": "<p>Rebates are discounts drug makers give PBMs to get their drug onto the <b>formulary</b> — the list of covered drugs and prices the PBM maintains for payers. Where a drug faces lots of competition, manufacturers may offer bigger rebates to stay on the list, at the cost of lower net revenue.</p>", "p": 206}, {"id": "ind-u4-10", "q": "What type of services do contract research organizations (CROs) provide?", "a": "<p>CROs run clinical trials for pharma and biotech companies on an outsourced, contract basis — managing trials and other specialised tasks to bring a drug or device to market and win approval faster. Clients get expertise for these high-stakes trials safely and cost-effectively, without hiring permanent specialist staff.</p>", "p": 206}]}, {"id": "ind-u4-l2", "title": "Pharma, generics & biotech valuation", "pages": "206–209", "cards": [{"id": "ind-u4-11", "q": "What is the difference between a branded and generic pharma company?", "a": "<p><b>Generics</b> (e.g. Teva) sell copies of drugs once the original patents expire. Because the products are commoditised, they compete on price, mainly by winning high-volume supply contracts. R&amp;D is lower (fewer trials), but sales &amp; marketing to pharmacies is higher, since winning those contracts is the game.</p><p><b>Branded pharma</b> (e.g. Novartis) has <b>patent exclusivity</b>, so it competes on innovation and quality reputation rather than price.</p>", "p": 206}, {"id": "ind-u4-12", "q": "What are the two pathways for a generics drug being approved?", "a": "<p><b>Paragraph III</b> (the standard route): wait for the patent to expire, then launch alongside other generics.</p><p><b>Paragraph IV:</b> challenge the patent <b>before</b> it expires. If successful, the first filer can win <b>180 days of exclusivity</b> before other generics can enter. Most generics companies use this if they think they have grounds to challenge.</p>", "p": 207}, {"id": "ind-u4-13", "q": "Let’s say you're valuing a pharmaceutical company selling drugs approved for therapeutic usage. What would the basic drivers of revenue be?", "a": "<div class=\"formula\">Revenue ≈ Number of patients × Average dosage × Average price</div><p>i.e. how many patients use the drug, the typical dose prescribed, and the average price.</p>", "p": 207}, {"id": "ind-u4-14", "q": "What assumption would you look at to value a clinical-stage biotechnology company?", "a": "<p>A biotech is a portfolio of drug candidates, each unique, so value them <b>separately</b>: forecast each candidate’s free cash flows, discount to NPV, and add them up. Biotechs burn cash, so the current <b>cash balance</b> is also key to equity value.</p><p>Key assumptions:</p><ul><li><b>Pipeline:</b> how many products, and what development phase each is in; whether there’s a discovery platform of pre-clinical research.</li><li><b>Peak sales opportunity</b> in the therapeutic area — often the single most important assumption. After peak, sales grow modestly until patent expiry / <b>loss of exclusivity</b>.</li><li><b>Indication:</b> the specific condition the drug is approved to treat (a subset of the therapeutic area).</li><li><b>Time to completion</b> of trials → <b>launch date</b>, then an <b>uptake curve</b> ramping each year to peak sales.</li><li><b>Probability of success</b> to risk-adjust the revenue (next card).</li></ul>", "p": 207}, {"id": "ind-u4-15", "q": "What does the “probability of success” rate refer to in receiving FDA approval?", "a": "<p>The likelihood a drug candidate makes it through trials to FDA approval — which varies by therapeutic area, but is low overall. In a biotech revenue build, each product’s revenue is multiplied by its <b>cumulative probability of success (POS)</b> across the remaining phases:</p><div class=\"formula\">Risk-adjusted revenue = Unadjusted revenue × Cumulative POS</div><p>E.g. 60% chance of passing Phase 2 × 50% for Phase 3 × 90% for approval → cumulative POS of 27%.</p>", "p": 207}, {"id": "ind-u4-16", "q": "Provide an overview of the path to becoming an approved therapy by the FDA.", "a": "<ol><li><b>Pre-clinical:</b> lab and animal testing to see whether it’s safe to test in humans and at what dose.</li><li><b>Phase 0</b> (optional): tiny exploratory study, usually under ~15 people at very low doses.</li><li><b>Phase 1:</b> safety — find the highest dose without serious side effects (~20–80 people).</li><li><b>Phase 2:</b> does it work? Test efficacy and dosing (a few dozen to 300+ people).</li><li><b>Phase 3:</b> is it better? Compare against existing treatments (several hundred to ~3,000 people).</li><li><b>NDA</b> (or BLA for biologics): formal application for approval. The FDA has ~60 days to decide whether to accept it for review, then reviews all the data, labelling and manufacturing sites before deciding.</li><li><b>Phase 4:</b> post-approval monitoring of safety and effectiveness in the wider population.</li></ol><p class=\"muted\">The book labels the FDA review itself “Phase 4”; normally Phase 4 means post-marketing studies after approval.</p>", "p": 208}, {"id": "ind-u4-17", "q": "How would you build a revenue model for a recently launched generic drug company?", "a": "<p>The two drivers are <b>recent launches</b> and the <b>pipeline</b>. For each generic, use the branded drug’s pre-expiry sales and pricing as a benchmark for market size. It’s usually a <b>top-down</b> build:</p><ol><li><b>Market volume:</b> total units (e.g. bottles × capsules per bottle) and price per bottle. Grow it slowly — roughly in line with population growth.</li><li><b>Generic share of the market:</b> the brand keeps some share at first, then loses it as PBMs push conversion. Assume a high generic capture (~85%) that rises over time — this is for <b>all</b> generics combined.</li><li><b>Number of competitors:</b> the key variable. Each new entrant cuts volume and price — a reasonable assumption is ~10% price erosion per new competitor — until the market is unprofitable and entry stops.</li><li><b>Company share:</b> simplest to split evenly among competitors, but reliable suppliers can win better pharmacy contracts and more share.</li><li><b>Revenue</b> = company volume × estimated price (e.g. from GoodRx or known negotiated prices).</li></ol>", "p": 208}, {"id": "ind-u4-18", "q": "On average, how does the pricing of generics compare to branded drugs?", "a": "<p>Generic prices are typically around <b>15–20% of the branded price</b> — and keep falling as more generic competitors enter.</p>", "p": 209}, {"id": "ind-u4-19", "q": "How would projecting revenue from drugs in the pipeline differ from recently launched generics?", "a": "<p>Pipeline products should be <b>risk-weighted</b> by the probability they make it through development and win FDA approval (for generics, bioequivalence approval). That captures the risk of manufacturing or quality-control problems, which is higher for complex drugs (e.g. extended-release or injectables).</p>", "p": 209}]}, {"id": "ind-u4-l3", "title": "Hospitals, payers & health plans", "pages": "209–211", "cards": [{"id": "ind-u4-20", "q": "How would you build a revenue model for a hospital?", "a": "<p><b>1. Footprint:</b> number of hospitals and freestanding outpatient surgery centres, grown mainly through <b>acquisitions</b> (reimbursement rates are largely out of a hospital’s control, so M&amp;A is the most reliable growth route).</p><p><b>2. Utilisation data:</b> licensed beds (and weighted average beds), admissions (inpatient volume), equivalent admissions, average length of stay and average daily census → occupancy rate.</p><p><b>3. Payer mix:</b> reimbursement differs by payer, so split revenue by Medicare, Managed Medicare, Medicaid, Managed Medicaid and commercial insurers.</p><p><b>4. Existing hospitals (owned &gt;1 year):</b> hospitals × beds per hospital × occupancy rate (with annual improvement) = average daily census; × 365 = total annual patient days. Split into outpatient volume and inpatient days; inpatient days ÷ average length of stay = inpatient admissions; add outpatient volume to get <b>equivalent admissions</b>. Multiply by <b>revenue per equivalent admission</b> (grown each year).</p><p><b>5. Acquired hospitals:</b> new hospitals × beds per hospital, then the same steps — usually with lower occupancy at first, improving over time.</p>", "p": 209}, {"id": "ind-u4-21", "q": "How do you calculate the equivalent admissions of a hospital?", "a": "<p>Equivalent admissions are a proxy for combined inpatient <b>and</b> outpatient volume, scaling admissions up by how much of revenue comes from outpatients:</p><div class=\"formula\">Equivalent admissions = Admissions × (Total inpatient + outpatient revenue ÷ Inpatient revenue)</div><p>E.g. 10,000 admissions with inpatient revenue 60% of the total → 10,000 ÷ 0.6 ≈ 16,667 equivalent admissions.</p>", "p": 210}, {"id": "ind-u4-22", "q": "What does the average occupancy rate represent, and how is it calculated?", "a": "<p>How well a hospital uses its capacity — a key driver of profitability.</p><div class=\"formula\">Occupancy rate = Average daily census ÷ Weighted average number of beds</div><p>Targets depend on the hospital and location, but ~<b>75%</b> is often cited as the most profitable level, and ~<b>85%</b> as “peak profitability” for high-end urban hospitals.</p>", "p": 210}, {"id": "ind-u4-23", "q": "What are the differences between Medicare vs. Managed Medicare and Medicaid vs. Managed Medicaid?", "a": "<p><b>Medicare:</b> federal coverage (mainly for people 65+), run directly by the government.</p><p><b>Managed Medicare (Medicare Advantage):</b> private managed care organisations (e.g. Humana, Aetna) contract with the government to cover Medicare-eligible people. Costs and benefits differ and may include extras not in Original Medicare, such as prescription drug coverage (Part D).</p><p><b>Medicaid:</b> coverage for low-income people, run at <b>state</b> level, so benefits vary by state.</p><p><b>Managed Medicaid:</b> states contract their Medicaid programmes out to MCOs, increasingly so because they’ve found MCOs more cost-effective with better service.</p>", "p": 210}, {"id": "ind-u4-24", "q": "What are managed care organizations (MCOs)?", "a": "<p>The non-government payers in the US — health insurers offering managed care plans. They earn <b>premiums</b> and <b>administrative fees</b> from customers (employers and individuals buying coverage).</p>", "p": 211}, {"id": "ind-u4-25", "q": "What impact did Medicaid expansion have on hospital reimbursements?", "a": "<p>Expanding Medicaid to more low-income people was central to the <b>Affordable Care Act (ACA)</b>. Medicaid pays the lowest reimbursement rates of any payer, but expansion still helped hospitals overall, because many patients who had been <b>uninsured</b> now had coverage. The trade-off: more oversight, outcome-based payments and penalties for non-compliance.</p>", "p": 211}, {"id": "ind-u4-26", "q": "How do you calculate the medical loss ratio (MLR) and why is it tracked?", "a": "<p>The share of premiums an insurer spends on medical claims and care-quality improvements.</p><div class=\"formula\">MLR = Medical costs ÷ Premium revenue</div><p>An 80% MLR means 20 cents of each premium dollar goes to overhead and profit. The ACA set minimum MLRs (80% for individual/small group, 85% for large group plans) so premiums are mostly spent on care rather than marketing and admin.</p>", "p": 211}, {"id": "ind-u4-27", "q": "What are some of the main health plans used today?", "a": "<ul><li>Preferred provider organisation (PPO)</li><li>Health maintenance organisation (HMO)</li><li>Point-of-service plan (POS)</li><li>Indemnity (fee-for-service) plans</li><li>Health savings account (HSA)</li></ul>", "p": 211}, {"id": "ind-u4-28", "q": "What are preferred provider organizations (PPOs)?", "a": "<p>Arrangements where doctors and facilities agree with an insurer (or third party) to treat the plan’s members at <b>discounted</b> rates. Payers steer patient volume to those providers in return. PPOs are by far the most common form of US managed care, and members can usually also see out-of-network providers at higher cost.</p>", "p": 211}, {"id": "ind-u4-29", "q": "What are health maintenance organizations (HMOs)?", "a": "<p>A plan covering care only within a network of contracted doctors and providers, for a monthly or annual premium. Members must first see their <b>primary care physician</b>, who refers them to specialists. Premiums are lower than traditional insurance because providers get patients steered to them; the downside is restricted access.</p>", "p": 211}]}, {"id": "ind-u4-l4", "title": "Plan types & integrated care", "pages": "212–213", "cards": [{"id": "ind-u4-30", "q": "What are point-of-service plans (POS)?", "a": "<p>A hybrid of HMO and PPO. Like an HMO, members have an in-network primary care physician; like a PPO, they can go out of network, with benefits depending on whether they use in- or out-of-network providers. POS plans are a small share of the market.</p>", "p": 212}, {"id": "ind-u4-31", "q": "What do indemnity healthcare plans involve?", "a": "<p>“Fee-for-service” plans that give patients the most freedom — they can see any doctor or specialist. After paying an annual deductible and any out-of-pocket costs, the patient submits a claim and the insurer pays a percentage of the charges, based on the <b>usual, customary and reasonable (UCR)</b> fees of comparable providers.</p>", "p": 212}, {"id": "ind-u4-32", "q": "What do usual, customary and reasonable (UCR) fees refer to?", "a": "<p>The amount an insurer considers reasonable for a service, based on what providers in the same area typically charge for the same or similar service. It varies by geography. If a provider charges more than the UCR amount, the patient may have to pay the difference.</p>", "p": 212}, {"id": "ind-u4-33", "q": "Could you explain what issue an integrated delivery network (IDN) is attempting to fix?", "a": "<p>An IDN is a network of healthcare facilities owned by one parent, providing a continuum of care across specialties. It tackles problems in today’s system: <b>misaligned incentives</b>, inefficiency (poor coordination and communication that raise costs) and <b>fragmentation</b>.</p><p>The ideal is coordinated care with aligned economics, better use of resources, better clinical outcomes and better service — organisations pooling assets and specialties, often through vertical integration, to care for patients comprehensively and efficiently.</p>", "p": 212}, {"id": "ind-u4-34", "q": "What does interoperability mean in healthcare?", "a": "<p>The ability of different IT systems and software to communicate and share data — so a patient’s records move seamlessly between clinics, labs, hospitals and pharmacies. It makes care more efficient, improves the patient experience, lowers costs and reduces errors.</p>", "p": 212}, {"id": "ind-u4-35", "q": "What is a physician-hospital organization (PHO)?", "a": "<p>A joint venture owned by a hospital and its physicians that contracts with managed care plans and coordinates services under a formal partnership. Integration varies by contract, and since hospitals’ and physicians’ economic incentives aren’t fully aligned, disagreements can limit cooperation.</p>", "p": 213}, {"id": "ind-u4-36", "q": "What are physician organizations (POs)?", "a": "<p>The traditional model: physician groups and hospitals coexist with <b>no contractual integration</b>. It’s under pressure from patients, regulators and managed care, and may not last — as reform pushes providers to integrate and align incentives, a shift toward PHO-type models is expected.</p>", "p": 213}, {"id": "ind-u4-37", "q": "What is a medical service organization (MSO)?", "a": "<p>An organisation that handles management, administrative and operational work for medical practices — billing and finance, HR, recruiting, regulatory compliance. It isn’t licensed to practise medicine, but by taking on time-consuming tasks it encourages hospital–physician integration.</p>", "p": 213}, {"id": "ind-u4-38", "q": "What is an independent practice association (IPA)?", "a": "<p>An association of independent licensed physicians that contracts collectively with HMOs and other payers, often at discounted fee-for-service rates. It funnels patients to member physicians’ offices, boosting their revenue, while the IPA processes claims under the contract terms.</p>", "p": 213}, {"id": "ind-u4-39", "q": "What does a partially integrated medical group (PIMG) mean?", "a": "<p>A step towards an integrated system, but not full integration. Several practices merge into a single legal entity, usually with central governance — but each practice keeps operational independence, its own identity and its own income allocation.</p>", "p": 213}, {"id": "ind-u4-40", "q": "What does a fully integrated medical group (FIMG) include?", "a": "<p>One end goal of integrated delivery: a single legal entity under centralised governance, operating under <b>one common name</b>, with fully integrated organisation and workflow — unified administration, clinical systems and a single income distribution system.</p>", "p": 213}]}, {"id": "ind-u4-l5", "title": "Provider payments, telehealth & trends", "pages": "213–216", "cards": [{"id": "ind-u4-41", "q": "Explain the 3 rd party business model in how providers are paid in the healthcare industry.", "a": "<p>Providers are paid through the patient’s <b>co-pay</b> at the time of care, plus fees from <b>payers</b>. After treatment, the provider submits a claim listing the services (by service code) and the reimbursement due.</p><p>To control costs, payers send claims to an internal <b>payment integrity</b> team or an outside HCIT firm to check for errors and incorrect billing. The adjusted claim goes back to the payer, which reimburses at <b>contracted rates</b> — e.g. hospitals in a PPO usually have 1–3 year contracts setting terms; physicians are paid off a fee schedule, often based on Medicare rates.</p>", "p": 213}, {"id": "ind-u4-42", "q": "Why is payment integrity such a point-of-conflict in the healthcare industry?", "a": "<p>Payment integrity means making sure claims are paid in the <b>right amount</b>. Firms review and dispute claims for insurers and Medicare, and are paid a percentage of the errors they find — e.g. arguing a patient was wrongly admitted as an inpatient when outpatient treatment would have sufficed. That pits payers against providers.</p><p>It’s hard to do well: there’s still lots of manual work despite automation, increasingly complex treatments make “medically necessary” harder to judge, and the sheer volume of claims makes anomalies hard to catch. AI looks promising as a solution.</p>", "p": 214}, {"id": "ind-u4-43", "q": "What is telemedicine and how does the process work?", "a": "<p>Treating patients remotely via secure (HIPAA-compliant) video. Patients discuss symptoms with a qualified provider, get a diagnosis and find out whether an in-person visit is needed — cutting unnecessary visits and costs, and making care more convenient.</p><p>COVID pushed it mainstream: many non-COVID patients avoided hospitals for fear of infection, Teladoc became a major beneficiary, and funding for telehealth start-ups surged in 2020. Benefits include better outcomes, more patient engagement (more frequent contact), wider access and lower costs.</p>", "p": 214}, {"id": "ind-u4-44", "q": "What are some limitations of telemedicine?", "a": "<p>It only works for <b>non-urgent</b> matters. Anything needing hands-on treatment or imaging (e.g. X-rays) can’t be handled remotely, so it’s best for routine issues and follow-ups after an in-person visit. A side benefit: it frees in-person capacity for urgent patients and gives overstretched medical staff more breathing room.</p>", "p": 214}, {"id": "ind-u4-45", "q": "What is utilization management in the healthcare context?", "a": "<p>Regularly reviewing how hospital and facility resources are used, to manage costs — and assessing quality of care to see where to adjust (understanding demand, focus areas and inefficiencies) while staying financially efficient. Telemedicine and workflow automation are expected to help lower facility costs.</p>", "p": 215}, {"id": "ind-u4-46", "q": "What is the difference between onsite and near-site healthcare, and what benefits do they provide to employees and employers?", "a": "<p>Employer clinics help tackle rising healthcare costs and employees’ chronic conditions, both of which cause lost work time. Employees spend less time away getting preventive care or treatment, and the benefit helps recruit staff.</p><p><b>Onsite clinics</b> are at the workplace and require big upfront investment, so mostly large corporations run them.</p><p><b>Near-site clinics</b> are run by third parties, needing far less investment, so smaller employers use them. They can actually be <b>more</b> convenient, with multiple locations to choose from, rather than having to travel to one onsite clinic.</p>", "p": 215}, {"id": "ind-u4-47", "q": "Talk to me about the rising trend of D2C startups in the pharmaceutical industry.", "a": "<p>Patients can now go online, consult a doctor on a range of conditions and get a prescription delivered — one of healthcare’s fastest-growing segments.</p><p>Start-ups like PillPack (bought by Amazon), Capsule, NowRx, Hims &amp; Hers, Ro and Blink Health are disrupting retail pharmacy with cheap, fast prescription delivery. Others like Truepill and Surescripts provide infrastructure. Growth has been helped by the de-stigmatisation of areas like birth control, erectile dysfunction, hair loss and acne, and consumers wanting more privacy.</p>", "p": 215}, {"id": "ind-u4-48", "q": "Tell me about a healthcare startup you're closely following.", "a": "<p>Pick one you genuinely know. The book’s example is <b>Truepill</b> (founded 2016), a B2B pharmacy API and fulfilment company often called the “AWS for pharmacies”.</p><ul><li>It powers D2C brands (e.g. Nurx, Hims, Lemonaid) and digital health firms, handling logistics, shipping and dispensing so they benefit from its scale and footprint.</li><li>Its licensed clinicians can diagnose and prescribe via telemedicine and clinics, and its pharmacies ship cash-pay or insurance-billed medication to patients.</li><li>Its API gives clients real-time data on fulfilment volume, on-time rates, delivery status and delays.</li></ul><p>Structure your answer as: what they do, who the customers are, why it’s a good business, and the risks (e.g. a crowded D2C market).</p>", "p": 215}, {"id": "ind-u4-49", "q": "From a financial perspective, how were hospitals and healthcare facilities affected by the COVID-19 pandemic?", "a": "<p>Badly. Revenue and profits fell as elective surgeries were postponed and accidents and non-COVID illnesses dropped. With high fixed costs, hospitals went into urgent cost-cutting — layoffs and reduced hours for non-COVID staff — and saw significant margin compression.</p><p>Meanwhile, digital infrastructure, diagnostics and testing, and telemedicine grew. Hospitals and clinics, notoriously slow to adopt technology and running on outdated systems, had to invest — expected to drive a big share of healthcare IT spending growth.</p>", "p": 216}]}], "ind-u5": [{"id": "ind-u5-l1", "title": "Consumer & retail basics", "pages": "217–219", "cards": [{"id": "ind-u5-1", "q": "Could you name some ongoing trends in the consumer goods industry?", "a": "<ul><li>Convergence of physical stores and e-commerce</li><li>Rapid product development and consumer testing</li><li>Data-driven, targeted marketing (e.g. personalised social ads)</li><li>Direct-to-consumer (DTC) business models</li><li>Subscription payment plans</li><li>Demand for label transparency — origin, how it’s made, health and sustainability</li><li>Same-day / one-day delivery, and click-and-collect</li><li>DTC brands partnering with physical retailers</li></ul>", "p": 217}, {"id": "ind-u5-2", "q": "Which types of valuation multiples are often used for consumer and retail companies?", "a": "<p>Mostly standard, given how mature the industry is: <b>P/E</b> and <b>EV/EBITDA</b>.</p><p>Retail-specific: <b>EV/EBITDAR</b>, where R adds back rent/lease costs. That puts retailers that rent their stores on the same footing as ones that own them — otherwise renters would look cheaper.</p><p>Operating-metric multiples: EV/number of stores, EV/sales per square foot, EV/sales per store, EV/same-store sales. For e-commerce and marketplaces: <b>EV/GMV</b> (gross merchandise volume).</p>", "p": 217}, {"id": "ind-u5-3", "q": "What unit economics drive revenue in the consumer goods industry?", "a": "<p>Revenue splits into <b>volume × price</b>.</p><p><b>Volume:</b> number of stores, daily customers per store, items per order, transactions per day.</p><p><b>Price:</b> depends on quality, brand reputation and positioning vs competitors. Most retailers have little pricing power and must price near the market.</p><p>So outside premium brands, most consumer companies grow through <b>volume</b> — better sales and marketing, more stores and geographic expansion.</p>", "p": 217}, {"id": "ind-u5-4", "q": "What is the most significant cost driver for consumer product companies?", "a": "<p><b>Advertising and marketing</b> — a uniquely large share of costs in consumer products. Ads are the main way to talk to customers, and marketing shapes how they see the brand. Done well, it drives sales and pricing power. Building an emotional connection is key to loyalty — e.g. Patagonia’s environmental and social initiatives.</p>", "p": 217}, {"id": "ind-u5-5", "q": "What are the two main retail channels to sell products to customers?", "a": "<p><b>Brick-and-mortar:</b> physical stores customers visit in person.</p><p><b>E-commerce:</b> online and mobile sales.</p><p>The two have converged — e.g. Walmart building out online shopping to compete with Amazon.</p>", "p": 218}, {"id": "ind-u5-6", "q": "If you were a seller, would you prefer to sell your products on a marketplace or personal e- commerce store?", "a": "<p>It depends on the brand.</p><p><b>Marketplace</b> (Amazon, eBay, AliExpress): built-in trust, security and traffic; buyers can compare products side by side. Sellers spend little to attract visitors. The cost is <b>commission</b> — typically 5–15% per sale.</p><p><b>Own e-commerce store:</b> more work and marketing/SEO spend to drive traffic, with no guarantee it pays off. But you control the experience, pay no commission (just hosting fees), and the brand is the only focus.</p><p>Brands with an existing following often avoid marketplaces: crowded with lower-tier brands and “sponsored” listings next to their products, marketplaces can make them look less premium.</p>", "p": 218}, {"id": "ind-u5-7", "q": "For a marketplace company, which KPIs would you pay the closest attention to?", "a": "<ul><li><b>Gross merchandise volume (GMV):</b> total value of goods sold through the platform over a period.</li><li><b>Average order value (AOV):</b> total sales ÷ number of orders.</li><li><b>Take rate:</b> the % of GMV the marketplace keeps as revenue — typically ~10–30% for established marketplaces.</li></ul><div class=\"formula\">Marketplace revenue = GMV × Take rate</div>", "p": 218}, {"id": "ind-u5-8", "q": "Explain how the “Amazon Effect” has impacted the entire retail industry.", "a": "<p>Amazon’s dominance has reshaped retail and raised customers’ expectations of online shopping:</p><ul><li><b>Shipping speed:</b> Prime’s 1–2 day delivery set a new standard that squeezes other sellers’ margins.</li><li><b>Free returns:</b> sellers now usually have to cover return shipping (and offer free delivery), losing leverage.</li></ul>", "p": 218}, {"id": "ind-u5-9", "q": "Why have many e-commerce platforms opened brick-and-mortar locations?", "a": "<ul><li><b>Emotional connection:</b> online sales are higher-margin and convenient, but it’s hard to build a bond with customers purely online. In-person experiences help.</li><li><b>Speed and convenience:</b> buying online and picking up in person (click-and-collect, curbside pickup in 2020) is the fastest way to get a product.</li><li><b>Cheaper returns:</b> returning online purchases at a local store saves shipping costs. Partnerships help too — e.g. Amazon returns accepted at Kohl’s.</li></ul>", "p": 219}, {"id": "ind-u5-10", "q": "Many DTC companies have recently established partnerships with brick-and-mortar store chains and began opening up physical stores. What is the reasoning behind this trend?", "a": "<p>DTC brands have realised physical retail complements online. A <b>multi-channel</b> strategy maximises exposure to different kinds of customers — e.g. Quip (toothbrushes) partnering with Target.</p><p>Many people like DTC with fast shipping, but some want to see a product in person before buying, even with free returns. “Buy online, pick up in store” (BOPIS) has also made a comeback. Beyond partnerships, brands like Casper, Warby Parker and Everlane have opened and expanded their own stores.</p>", "p": 219}]}, {"id": "ind-u5-l2", "title": "Channels, CPG & retail KPIs", "pages": "219–222", "cards": [{"id": "ind-u5-11", "q": "What are some benefits that brick-and-mortar stores have over e-commerce?", "a": "<ul><li><b>The shopping experience:</b> done well, it builds an emotional connection with the brand. Struggling retailers like Macy’s have tried to make stores more experiential — in-store VR, mobile checkout, modern food vendors, better lighting and family-friendly layouts.</li><li><b>Fewer returns:</b> around ~30% of online purchases are returned vs ~9% in store, since customers see the product first — and online sellers usually pay return shipping.</li></ul>", "p": 219}, {"id": "ind-u5-12", "q": "Are multichannel and omnichannel marketing synonymous?", "a": "<p>No. Both use several channels to reach buyers — marketing channels (TV, social, billboards, website) and sales channels (email, calls, live chat, referrals).</p><p><b>Multichannel:</b> each channel runs separately, with its own message, not linked to the others.</p><p><b>Omnichannel:</b> channels are <b>integrated</b> into one seamless experience designed from the customer’s point of view, with a consistent message across all of them.</p>", "p": 219}, {"id": "ind-u5-13", "q": "Tell me about the significance of PayPal’s acquisition of Honey.", "a": "<p>In late 2019 PayPal bought <b>Honey</b>, a browser extension that finds coupons and discounts while you shop online, for about $4bn — PayPal’s largest acquisition at the time, and a remarkable price for a browser extension.</p><p>The rationale: integrate Honey’s extension and shopping app with PayPal and Venmo checkout to make shopping simpler (e.g. buying across multiple sites at the lowest prices). With 17m+ monthly active users, Honey would strengthen PayPal’s consumer footprint and engagement against Apple Pay and Square’s Cash App. CEO Dan Schulman called it one of the most transformative deals in PayPal’s history.</p>", "p": 220}, {"id": "ind-u5-14", "q": "Could you define what consumer-packaged-goods (CPG) and retail are?", "a": "<p><b>CPG</b> (fast-moving consumer goods): everyday products used up and replaced quickly — toilet paper, shampoo, soda, soap. Highly competitive: brands fight for limited shelf space, products are often commoditised, and success depends on loyalty and familiarity — hence “copycat” store brands placed next to Coca-Cola at a discount.</p><p><b>Retail</b> is the business of <b>selling</b> products to consumers. CPG is a product category; retail is the sales process. Monthly retail sales data, tracking spending on durable and non-durable goods, is closely watched as a gauge of the economy.</p>", "p": 220}, {"id": "ind-u5-15", "q": "What is social commerce, and how is it different from social media marketing?", "a": "<p><b>Social media marketing</b> uses social platforms to promote products and send customers to the company’s own site. <b>Social commerce</b> lets customers <b>buy directly inside</b> the social app — e.g. Facebook Shops and Instagram Shopping.</p><p>Platforms benefit because businesses buy more ads, drawn by huge audiences, targeting data, influencers and features like live shopping and product tags. Messenger, Instagram Direct and WhatsApp let sellers answer questions in chat, keeping the experience seamless.</p>", "p": 220}, {"id": "ind-u5-16", "q": "How are chatbots being used in the retail industry?", "a": "<p>Pre-programmed AI assistants on websites that handle customer service without a human. They answer common questions instantly and cut costs once built. But they can’t answer everything, so a human is usually on standby, and many customers still prefer a person after frustrating early chatbots.</p>", "p": 221}, {"id": "ind-u5-17", "q": "What is the difference between a discretionary product and a non-discretionary product?", "a": "<p><b>Non-discretionary (consumer staples):</b> everyday necessities bought regardless of income or the economy — water, soap, toothpaste, shampoo, razors. Less cyclical. E.g. Unilever.</p><p><b>Discretionary:</b> pricier non-essentials bought more when the economy is strong — new cars, TVs, phone upgrades.</p><p>In recessions, staples keep selling while consumers put <b>discretionary</b> purchases on hold.</p><p class=\"muted\">The book says “non-discretionary” in that last line — it means discretionary.</p>", "p": 221}, {"id": "ind-u5-18", "q": "What are some common characteristics of CPGs?", "a": "<ul><li>Low average selling prices</li><li>Essential to users</li><li>Used up and thrown away quickly</li><li>Frequent repurchases (often weekly or monthly)</li><li>Little customisation</li></ul>", "p": 221}, {"id": "ind-u5-19", "q": "What is the difference between CPG and durable goods?", "a": "<p><b>CPG:</b> used often and replaced quickly — a short replacement cycle.</p><p><b>Durable goods:</b> bought rarely, much more expensive and last for years — TVs, washing machines, furniture, fridges.</p><p>Another big difference is <b>decision time</b>: people may research a laptop for weeks, but pick a CPG item in minutes.</p>", "p": 221}, {"id": "ind-u5-20", "q": "What does it mean when a product has become commoditized?", "a": "<p>Competing products are barely differentiated, so competition is on <b>price alone</b> — a race to the bottom to sell cheapest.</p>", "p": 221}, {"id": "ind-u5-21", "q": "Name a few KPIs that many retail chains report in their financial statements.", "a": "<ul><li><b>Sales per square foot</b> = net sales ÷ selling space — how efficiently floor space is used vs peers.</li><li><b>Sales per employee</b> = total sales ÷ employees — whether the sales strategy and staffing are working (stores also use it to spot struggling staff).</li><li><b>Conversion rate</b> = transactions ÷ visitors — how many visitors actually buy.</li><li><b>Same-store sales growth</b> = (current period sales ÷ prior-year period sales − 1) × 100 for stores open over a year — isolates performance from new openings, and lets you see which stores beat or miss expectations and why.</li><li><b>Average transaction value</b> = total revenue ÷ transactions — the effectiveness of product placement, promotions and upselling.</li></ul>", "p": 222}]}, {"id": "ind-u5-l3", "title": "Consumer trends & resilient segments", "pages": "222–224", "cards": [{"id": "ind-u5-22", "q": "Tell me what the trend of voice commerce is and its use cases in retail.", "a": "<p>Using voice recognition and AI assistants to find and buy products. Instead of searching on a laptop and reading reviews, a shopper can ask a voice assistant for options, hear how they differ, state their needs and tell it to place the order.</p>", "p": 222}, {"id": "ind-u5-23", "q": "What is the gig economy?", "a": "<p>Online platforms connecting independent contractors with short-term jobs (“gigs”), letting them work flexible hours instead of full-time employment. Also called the sharing economy, it covers freelancing, ride-hailing (Uber, Lyft), food delivery (Grubhub, DoorDash) and short-term rentals (Airbnb).</p>", "p": 222}, {"id": "ind-u5-24", "q": "Tell me about an ongoing trend in e-commerce that you're following.", "a": "<p>Pick one you know well. The book’s example: <b>pet e-commerce</b>.</p><ul><li><b>Pet humanisation</b>, led by millennials, has driven demand for premium pet food and products, with more transparent ingredients and higher quality.</li><li>Pets are one of the fastest-growing consumer categories and among the highest order-volume categories online.</li><li>COVID accelerated DTC pet delivery (e.g. <b>Chewy</b>): owners spent more time at home with pets, and many discovered the convenience of subscription orders.</li></ul>", "p": 223}, {"id": "ind-u5-25", "q": "Could you give me an example of how augmented reality is changing the consumer experience in the retail industry?", "a": "<p><b>Warby Parker</b> (founded 2010) first disrupted eyewear with Home Try-On, mailing sample frames to customers. It then launched <b>Virtual Try-On</b>, using the phone camera and AR to show customers wearing virtual frames. It maps the face with AI and recommends frames that suit the customer’s features.</p><p>It shows how AR can make online shopping feel more real. Similar features have spread to furniture and clothing start-ups, and incumbents have added them to their apps and sites.</p>", "p": 223}, {"id": "ind-u5-26", "q": "While retail is known for being one of the most cyclical industries, can you name one resilient product segment?", "a": "<p><b>Cosmetics and beauty.</b> Purchases are driven by <b>intangible</b> benefits (how people feel about themselves), so price matters less, and higher prices barely dent volumes. Products applied to the skin feel especially personal. Studies of past downturns show beauty routines are among the last things people cut.</p><p>So beauty is less discretionary than it looks: demand rises in expansions and holds steady in contractions — unusual for normally price-sensitive consumers.</p>", "p": 223}, {"id": "ind-u5-27", "q": "Why are luxury goods often not negatively affected during recessions?", "a": "<p>Luxury is less cyclical than people assume because of who buys it. High-income customers often don’t change their spending in a recession. Demand from aspirational buyers who can no longer afford it falls, but the core wealthy customers keep buying.</p>", "p": 223}, {"id": "ind-u5-28", "q": "How does pricing power in retail differ from other industries such as manufacturing?", "a": "<p>In retail, pricing power comes from <b>emotional, intangible</b> factors — brand trust and appeal — that are hard to quantify. In technical industries like manufacturing, the best-quality product tends to win over time. In retail, a well-marketed, lower-quality product with stronger brand appeal can outsell a better one.</p>", "p": 224}, {"id": "ind-u5-29", "q": "What makes the beauty products industry not just resilient to recessions but recurring in terms of revenue?", "a": "<p>Most consumer goods have a natural limit on how much people buy and use. Skincare and cosmetics are used up — face masks, lotions — and need constant replenishment, so purchases are highly <b>recurring</b>. That makes skincare one of the most profitable consumer categories.</p>", "p": 224}, {"id": "ind-u5-30", "q": "Besides cyclicality, why do many private equity investors stay away from retail?", "a": "<p>Retail growth is mostly <b>volume-based</b>, which is the least valuable kind: you must keep increasing sales and constantly adapt to changing tastes, rather than raising prices or upselling.</p><p>That said, adaptability, a strong brand and scale can become durable advantages — unit costs and capex needs fall, lifting gross margins. Still, most retail investments are structured as VC/growth equity rather than traditional LBOs.</p>", "p": 224}, {"id": "ind-u5-31", "q": "Tell me about the trend of connected fitness.", "a": "<p>Home equipment with built-in screens, classes and virtual trainers, led by <b>Peloton</b>, which took off during COVID — letting people train at home instead of the gym. Lululemon’s acquisition of <b>Mirror</b> was a notable deal.</p><p class=\"muted\">Since the book, demand fell sharply after lockdowns: Peloton’s value collapsed and Lululemon wrote down and wound down Mirror.</p>", "p": 224}, {"id": "ind-u5-32", "q": "What is the trend of athleisure wear?", "a": "<p>Clothing that blends workout, casual and work wear, especially popular with younger consumers. It’s become a bigger part of everyday wardrobes because it’s comfortable and versatile, and formal dress codes are fading (especially in tech). Lululemon is a leader.</p>", "p": 224}]}, {"id": "ind-u5-l4", "title": "Retail strategy, food & beverage", "pages": "224–226", "cards": [{"id": "ind-u5-33", "q": "What does the phrase “fast fashion” mean?", "a": "<p>Getting runway-style designs into stores as fast as possible to catch short-lived trends. Tastes change quickly with seasons and trends, and retailers like <b>Zara</b> pioneered putting trendy items on racks early. Quantities are often deliberately limited, creating a rush to buy — which means rapid restocking and higher margins.</p>", "p": 224}, {"id": "ind-u5-34", "q": "What is the secondhand market?", "a": "<p>Resale isn’t new, but it’s forecast to be one of retail’s fastest-growing segments as the <b>circular economy</b> spreads and the stigma of buying used clothing fades. Social marketplaces like <b>Poshmark</b> and <b>thredUP</b> let anyone list clothes for sale — essentially a closet clear-out.</p>", "p": 225}, {"id": "ind-u5-35", "q": "Often retail-brand companies such as Supreme can sell clothing that will be resold for significantly more. Why does Supreme not just raise their prices?", "a": "<p>Supreme’s model is built on <b>scarcity</b> and a premium image, and its customers are mostly young. Raise prices and many fans become window-shoppers instead of people queuing for days.</p><p>Keeping prices within reach — an upper-middle-class teenager can afford it, with supply the only constraint — makes the brand more visible and desirable. Ultra-premium brands are unknown to most people; Supreme is seen as premium yet recognised across all income levels. That positioning led to Carlyle buying a 50% stake at a $1bn valuation.</p><p class=\"muted\">VF Corp later bought Supreme for $2.1bn (2020), then sold it to EssilorLuxottica (2024).</p>", "p": 225}, {"id": "ind-u5-36", "q": "What is scrambled merchandising?", "a": "<p>Stocking a wide, unrelated mix of products to serve as many customers as possible and position the store as a one-stop shop — e.g. a pharmacy like CVS selling headphones and speakers.</p>", "p": 225}, {"id": "ind-u5-37", "q": "In the retail context, what does “channel surfing” mean?", "a": "<p>Also called <b>showrooming</b>: customers try products and get information in a store, then buy online instead — for a lower price, online-only discounts or lower tax.</p>", "p": 225}, {"id": "ind-u5-38", "q": "What type of differentiated business model did Dollar Shave Club have?", "a": "<p>An early <b>subscription DTC</b> model. Cheap, convenient razor subscriptions and transparent branding quickly took share from Gillette. Gillette responded with its own subscription kits, and new rivals like Harry’s entered men’s grooming.</p><p class=\"muted\">Unilever bought Dollar Shave Club for $1bn in 2016.</p>", "p": 225}, {"id": "ind-u5-39", "q": "Tell me about the trend of retailers using DOOH for their advertising.", "a": "<p><b>Digital out-of-home</b> advertising runs on electronic screens on buildings, highways, subways and bus stops. Like traditional billboards, it targets busy urban spots, but it’s <b>data-driven</b> — using AdTech like geo-fencing and personalisation to reach the right viewers, with higher engagement and conversion. E.g. a Nordstrom billboard that changes which clothes it shows based on the weather.</p>", "p": 225}, {"id": "ind-u5-40", "q": "What is a private label brand?", "a": "<p>A product made by a third-party manufacturer and sold under the retailer’s own brand — common in food, cosmetics and general merchandise. They’re usually cheaper and often mimic the market leader. E.g. Target, with 45+ private labels (such as Open Story luggage, launched 2020), which have boosted its growth and margins.</p>", "p": 226}, {"id": "ind-u5-41", "q": "Tell me about some trends related to the food industry.", "a": "<ul><li><b>Delivery and takeout apps:</b> Uber Eats, Grubhub, DoorDash — delivered by gig workers or collected.</li><li><b>Meal kits:</b> subscription boxes of ingredients that customers cook at home (HelloFresh, Blue Apron, Home Chef).</li><li><b>Plant-based alternatives</b> to meat and dairy, for perceived health and sustainability benefits (Beyond Meat, Impossible Foods).</li><li><b>Diet-specific menus:</b> e.g. Chipotle’s keto and paleo bowls.</li><li><b>Transparency and sustainability:</b> clear labels, sustainable packaging and ethical production.</li></ul>", "p": 226}, {"id": "ind-u5-42", "q": "Tell me about some trends in the beverage industry.", "a": "<ul><li><b>CBD-infused drinks:</b> hugely popular in 2019 for claimed benefits for anxiety, pain and sleep, while regulators scrambled to classify them.</li><li><b>Functional hybrid drinks:</b> multiple benefits in one product, e.g. coffee with protein (Kitu Super Coffee, REBBL).</li><li><b>Hard seltzer:</b> alcoholic sparkling water — flavourful and low-calorie, popular with younger drinkers (White Claw, Vizzy).</li></ul>", "p": 226}]}], "ind-u6": [{"id": "ind-u6-l1", "title": "Distress, alternatives & priority", "pages": "227–230", "cards": [{"id": "ind-u6-1", "q": "What are the most common reasons for a company to become distressed?", "a": "<ul><li><b>Liquidity problems:</b> the usual trigger is a missed interest or principal payment, typically after an unexpected deterioration in performance.</li><li><b>Accelerated payments:</b> some bonds have acceleration/“make-whole” provisions that make the full principal, premium and interest due early — controversial, but written into the indenture to protect lenders’ yield.</li><li><b>Failed LBOs:</b> too much leverage plus underperformance, so the company can’t carry its debt (e.g. Toys “R” Us, Chapter 11 in 2017).</li><li><b>Missed supplier payments:</b> suppliers are usually accommodating, but may cut off a company they see as a lost cause.</li><li><b>Covenant breaches:</b> a breach (e.g. a ratings downgrade trigger) can force a default.</li><li><b>Industry disruption:</b> products become obsolete (Blockbuster vs Netflix).</li><li><b>Black swan events:</b> e.g. the subprime crisis, or COVID-19, which pushed J.Crew, Brooks Brothers and Ruby Tuesday into bankruptcy.</li></ul>", "p": 227}, {"id": "ind-u6-2", "q": "What are a few typical signs that a debtor is planning to file for bankruptcy soon?", "a": "<p>A debtor that knows it must file will time it to preserve cash and flexibility — often just before a big payment is due. Warning signs:</p><ul><li>The revolver has been <b>fully drawn</b>.</li><li>Covenants have been breached, or soon will be.</li><li>Payments to suppliers and vendors are being <b>delayed</b>.</li></ul><p>Restructuring questions come up at Rx advisory firms, M&amp;A boutiques with Rx practices, corporate banking, PE and distressed debt hedge funds.</p>", "p": 227}, {"id": "ind-u6-3", "q": "Other than declaring bankruptcy, what alternatives does a company have to stay afloat?", "a": "<ul><li><b>Straight refinancing:</b> replace old debt with new — ideal but unrealistic, since few lenders will refinance a distressed company. And without a credible turnaround, it just delays (or worsens) the problem.</li><li><b>Raise equity:</b> few investors will buy shares in a company near bankruptcy, since equity gets nothing until debt is repaid in full. (Hertz tried to sell new shares after filing for bankruptcy; the offering was approved but quickly suspended.)</li><li><b>Out-of-court restructuring:</b> the most practical option — renegotiate with existing creditors: delayed payments, extended maturities (and lower coupons), switching to PIK interest, or even a “haircut” on principal and interest so the company can keep operating.</li></ul>", "p": 228}, {"id": "ind-u6-4", "q": "As a company nears becoming distressed, how might its accounts receivable, deferred revenue, and accounts payable balances change?", "a": "<ul><li><b>Deferred revenue rises:</b> companies with contracts or subscriptions sell future delivery for cash now (often at steep discounts) to raise liquidity.</li><li><b>Accounts payable rises:</b> DPO stretches out as the company can’t pay suppliers, even if it wants to.</li><li><b>Accounts receivable:</b> failing to collect may be part of the liquidity problem. Since A/R counts towards the borrowing base, the company may sell more on credit to boost it — and customers aware of the trouble may deliberately pay late in case it goes bankrupt.</li></ul>", "p": 228}, {"id": "ind-u6-5", "q": "What does operational restructuring involve?", "a": "<p>Many distressed companies see no viable turnaround and are liquidated, but others have a short window to fix the underlying problems. Operational restructuring means improving operations through <b>cost cuts, better working capital management and headcount reductions</b>, plus <b>asset restructuring</b> — selling unproductive, non-core assets or divisions to refocus on the core. Management often hires restructuring advisors or outside consultants to help.</p>", "p": 228}, {"id": "ind-u6-6", "q": "What is the difference between a voluntary and involuntary petition?", "a": "<p><b>Voluntary petition:</b> the debtor files itself — by far the most common. Filing first brings advantages, like choosing the jurisdiction, so debtors almost always file pre-emptively.</p><p><b>Involuntary petition:</b> rare — creditors force the company into bankruptcy, often after a technical default (broken covenants or other violations).</p>", "p": 228}, {"id": "ind-u6-7", "q": "Contrast an out of court restructuring with an in-court restructuring.", "a": "<p><b>Out of court</b> — negotiating with lenders and trade creditors without a court:</p><ul><li><b>Pros:</b> faster, cheaper and private, avoiding bankruptcy stigma (e.g. vendors demanding cash on delivery).</li><li><b>Cons:</b> every affected creditor must agree — nothing can be imposed — so the more parties, the harder it is (the <b>holdout problem</b>). No access to DIP financing, and it’s hard to attract new money without bankruptcy protections.</li></ul><p><b>In court</b> (bankruptcy) — overseen by a judge:</p><ul><li><b>Pros:</b> the only way to impose changes (or rejections) on leases, pensions and debt agreements; the <b>automatic stay</b> stops collection; holdouts are solved by grouping creditors into classes that share recoveries pro rata; access to DIP financing with priority status.</li><li><b>Cons:</b> legal fees drain cash when liquidity is scarce, and the process can take years — though pre-packs can be much faster.</li></ul>", "p": 229}, {"id": "ind-u6-8", "q": "What is the purpose of the automatic stay provision?", "a": "<p>When a company files for Chapter 11, the automatic stay temporarily stops creditors — lenders, collection agencies, government bodies — from pursuing collection or legal action, so the company can keep operating.</p><p>The filing date draws a line between <b>pre-petition</b> and <b>post-petition</b>. Pre-petition claims can’t be collected without court approval and are subject to compromise; post-petition liabilities get administrative priority over them.</p>", "p": 230}, {"id": "ind-u6-9", "q": "Which claim holders get priority in a bankruptcy?", "a": "<ol><li><b>“Super-priority” claims</b> — court-approved DIP financing, ranking even above regular secured claims.</li><li><b>Secured pre-petition claims</b> — secured by liens on assets; usually the highest recoveries.</li><li><b>Administrative claims</b> — legal, advisory and other professional fees from the bankruptcy, which must be paid for the process to work.</li><li><b>Priority unsecured claims</b> — e.g. certain accrued employee wages and taxes.</li><li><b>General unsecured claims (GUCs)</b> — bondholders, unsecured lenders and trade creditors.</li><li><b>Preferred and common equity</b> — gets whatever is left, which is usually nothing.</li></ol>", "p": 230}, {"id": "ind-u6-10", "q": "Following a bankruptcy filing, will the management team be replaced or remain on board?", "a": "<p>Usually the <b>same management stays</b>, unless there’s proven fraud or misconduct. They run day-to-day operations — and having made the decisions that led here, they arguably understand what went wrong better than anyone. A turnaround specialist is often brought in alongside them as extra support (an “insurance policy”).</p>", "p": 230}]}, {"id": "ind-u6-l2", "title": "The Chapter 11 process", "pages": "230–232", "cards": [{"id": "ind-u6-11", "q": "In Chapter 11, under which circumstances might a Court appoint a trustee to replace the existing CEO?", "a": "<p>A Chapter 11 trustee can take over day-to-day operations — not ideal, since they need time to learn the business. The court appoints one in two situations:</p><ol><li>Management engaged in <b>fraud, dishonesty</b> or criminal misconduct.</li><li>Replacing management is in the <b>“best interests”</b> of the company and stakeholders — a more subjective call, usually based on gross mismanagement and loss of stakeholder trust.</li></ol>", "p": 230}, {"id": "ind-u6-12", "q": "Can a decline in a company's share price and valuation cause it to become distressed?", "a": "<p>Not by itself. A falling share price reflects the market <b>anticipating</b> distress (which may be justified). As long as the company keeps paying its debt and doesn’t breach covenants, it can keep operating. The side effects can hurt, though: suppliers, vendors and customers may become reluctant to work with it, and raising capital gets harder.</p>", "p": 231}, {"id": "ind-u6-13", "q": "From an advisory standpoint, what are the two sides to a restructuring deal?", "a": "<p><b>Creditor side:</b> advising loan or bondholders, often a group of holders in one class who have banded together (frequently hedge funds or PE firms). The more parties, the longer agreement takes.</p><p><b>Debtor side:</b> advising the distressed company itself — a single client. The banker gets to know the business deeply and works on the turnaround.</p>", "p": 231}, {"id": "ind-u6-14", "q": "Broadly, what are the two main types of bankruptcies?", "a": "<p><b>Chapter 7:</b> liquidation. All assets are sold and proceeds distributed down the waterfall under the <b>absolute priority rule</b> — senior claims are paid in full before anything flows to juniors.</p><p><b>Chapter 11:</b> court-supervised <b>reorganisation</b>. The company puts together a plan of reorganisation (POR) identifying impaired classes; only impaired classes vote. Once the plan is approved, the company emerges with a new capital structure.</p>", "p": 231}, {"id": "ind-u6-15", "q": "What is a pre-pack in a bankruptcy?", "a": "<p>A <b>pre-packaged</b> bankruptcy: the plan is negotiated and agreed with the relevant creditors <b>before</b> filing. The Chapter 11 filing comes with the disclosure statement, proposed POR and agreements to support it. It needs enough creditor votes to pass, and can take as little as ~45 days. Pre-packs have become more common.</p>", "p": 231}, {"id": "ind-u6-16", "q": "Contrast the pre-pack vs. the traditional Chapter 11.", "a": "<p><b>Pre-pack:</b> key impaired creditors have already agreed a POR before filing, so the process is streamlined and the company can emerge within months.</p><p><b>Traditional (“free-fall”) Chapter 11:</b> starts from scratch, can take years, and is often messy, with creditors not on the same page. The pre-pack exists precisely to avoid that.</p>", "p": 231}, {"id": "ind-u6-17", "q": "What information is contained in a plan of reorganization (POR)?", "a": "<p>After filing, the goal is to emerge with an agreed POR. It sets out what happens to the debtor’s assets, liabilities and equity on exit — most importantly each claim class’s <b>expected recovery</b> and the <b>form of consideration</b> (cash, new debt, new equity).</p>", "p": 232}, {"id": "ind-u6-18", "q": "What are the four tests representing the minimum standards of fairness for a POR to be approved?", "a": "<ol><li><b>Best interests test:</b> recoveries under the POR must exceed what creditors would get in a Chapter 7 liquidation.</li><li><b>Good faith:</b> the plan must be proposed in good faith.</li><li><b>Feasibility:</b> the plan mustn’t be likely to lead to another restructuring or liquidation.</li><li><b>Cramdown:</b> even if an impaired class rejects the plan, the proponent can ask the court to confirm it anyway (if it meets the fairness requirements).</li></ol>", "p": 232}, {"id": "ind-u6-19", "q": "What type of creditor might prefer a Chapter 7 liquidation over a Chapter 11?", "a": "<p>A <b>secured creditor</b> confident of a 100% recovery under either chapter. Chapter 11 usually produces higher total recoveries, but that doesn’t matter to someone getting paid in full anyway — and Chapter 7 is faster.</p>", "p": 232}, {"id": "ind-u6-20", "q": "What role does the US Trustee have in Chapter 11 bankruptcies?", "a": "<p>The US Trustee (part of the Justice Department) oversees the administration of the case. It makes sure the debtor-in-possession follows operating and reporting rules and files reports and documents on time, and addresses fairness concerns from either debtor or creditors — protecting the integrity of the bankruptcy system.</p>", "p": 232}]}, {"id": "ind-u6-l3", "title": "Valuation & recoveries in bankruptcy", "pages": "232–234", "cards": [{"id": "ind-u6-21", "q": "How does valuation play a role in bankruptcies?", "a": "<p>Valuation is subjective but decides who gets what, so it’s fiercely contested.</p><p>Example: $300m of secured claims and $200m of unsecured claims. The POR reinstates the secured debt and converts unsecured claims into equity.</p><ul><li><b>If the court values the company at $400m:</b> equity is worth $100m ($400m − $300m) — less than the $200m unsecured claim — so unsecured creditors get <b>100% of the equity</b> and old equity is wiped out.</li><li><b>If it values it at $700m:</b> equity is worth $400m, so unsecured creditors are made whole with just <b>50%</b> of it ($200m ÷ $400m), and the other 50% goes to old equity.</li></ul><p>That’s why each class argues for the valuation that suits it, and the process encourages negotiation between classes.</p>", "p": 232}, {"id": "ind-u6-22", "q": "What is the relationship between a liquidation analysis and the \"best interests\" test?", "a": "<p>A key reason to run a liquidation analysis is to compare it with the POR. To pass the <b>best interests test</b>, the Chapter 11 plan must give creditors more than they’d get in a Chapter 7 liquidation. So the liquidation analysis sets the <b>floor</b> for creditor recoveries.</p>", "p": 233}, {"id": "ind-u6-23", "q": "During Chapter 11 restructuring, what does the cash flow test require?", "a": "<p>The <b>feasibility test</b>: the debtor must show it will stay solvent under the proposed POR — that the post-emergence capital structure is sustainable. Even a plan that benefits everyone can be rejected if the company is likely to need liquidation or another restructuring soon.</p><p>It uses a projection of cash flows, a debt schedule under the new terms, and the ending cash balance each period — and must show solvency not only in the base case but in a downside case too.</p>", "p": 233}, {"id": "ind-u6-24", "q": "What is the absolute priority rule, and why is it important?", "a": "<p>The APR says no lower-priority class gets anything until every higher class is paid in full. Classes are ranked and recoveries flow down in order.</p><p>In practice, recoveries often <b>deviate</b> from it: junior creditors or equity sometimes get something even when seniors aren’t fully repaid. Juniors can hold up the process or threaten litigation, so seniors may give them a small recovery to avoid the hassle.</p>", "p": 233}, {"id": "ind-u6-25", "q": "In bankruptcies, which class usually holds the largest claim?", "a": "<p>Usually the <b>general unsecured claims (GUCs)</b> — pre-petition trade creditors without priority (e.g. supplier payables), unsecured lenders, and holders of unsecured bonds and notes.</p>", "p": 233}, {"id": "ind-u6-26", "q": "What does the priority unsecured class involve?", "a": "<p>Unsecured claims that the Bankruptcy Code ranks <b>ahead of</b> general unsecured claims — e.g. certain accrued employee wages and certain taxes owed to the government.</p>", "p": 233}, {"id": "ind-u6-27", "q": "How would you value a distressed company?", "a": "<p>Both as a <b>going concern</b> and as a <b>liquidation</b>.</p><ul><li><b>Going concern:</b> a DCF, usually with a higher-than-normal cost of capital for the extra risk. Comps can be used too, but multiples from healthy peers will likely be too high.</li><li><b>Liquidation analysis:</b> value the business as a collection of assets that would be sold off.</li></ul>", "p": 233}, {"id": "ind-u6-28", "q": "What is the intent of liquidation analysis?", "a": "<p>To value the company assuming it will shut down and sell its assets. It counts mainly <b>tangible</b> assets — real estate, PP&amp;E, inventory, receivables — and excludes intangibles. Because it’s a fire sale needing cash quickly for creditors, assets are assumed to sell at a <b>discount</b> to fair value (recovery rates vary by asset type).</p>", "p": 234}, {"id": "ind-u6-29", "q": "When using the traditional valuation methodologies, why might valuing a distressed and non- distressed business differ?", "a": "<p>Same methods, but much more conservative:</p><ul><li>More of the value comes from the <b>early forecast years</b> than the terminal value.</li><li>Perpetuity growth is very low or even <b>negative</b> (implying a slow wind-down).</li><li>Exit multiples come from the <b>bottom</b> of the peer range.</li><li>Discount rates are much higher — often <b>15–25%+</b>.</li><li>Comps use the low end of peer multiples.</li></ul>", "p": 234}, {"id": "ind-u6-30", "q": "What is distressed debt?", "a": "<p>Bonds trading well below face value — roughly <b>below 80 cents on the dollar</b> — or loans trading at very wide spreads (roughly <b>+1,000bps</b> over the benchmark rate). It usually reflects an expected or perceived need for restructuring, often from operating problems plus high leverage. Distressed investors typically buy it from the original holders (banks, institutions).</p><p class=\"muted\">The book quotes spreads over LIBOR; loans now price off SOFR.</p>", "p": 234}]}, {"id": "ind-u6-l4", "title": "Distressed investing & DIP financing", "pages": "234–236", "cards": [{"id": "ind-u6-31", "q": "Would you rather own bank debt or bonds in a bankruptcy?", "a": "<p>It depends.</p><ul><li><b>Priority:</b> if the bank debt is secured and the bonds aren’t, the bank debt gets higher recoveries — secured creditors often recover in full, unsecured only partially. If neither is secured, they’re in theory <b>pari passu</b>.</li><li><b>Other factors:</b> bonds subordinated via an inter-creditor agreement recover less; and if a big chunk of bank debt matures before the filing and gets paid, bank recoveries rise.</li><li><b>Price:</b> as an investor, what matters is recovery vs price. If secured bank debt trades at 95 and unsecured bonds at 50, but you think the bonds will recover 70, the bonds are the better buy.</li></ul>", "p": 234}, {"id": "ind-u6-32", "q": "How does a distressed debt investor make money?", "a": "<p>By buying bonds, loans or trade claims trading <b>below their likely recovery</b> — and often accumulating enough to gain influence in negotiations and steer the restructuring in their favour.</p><p>E.g. a bond at 50 implies a ~50% expected recovery. Analysis might show that a seemingly senior claim isn’t really senior (because of its place in the corporate structure or problems with its lien), that liquidation would give bondholders a faster, safer recovery than a Chapter 11 burdened with DIP debt and heavy fees, or that some assets are undervalued. The investor buys at 50 and uses its seat at the table to push the judge and other creditors towards its view.</p>", "p": 235}, {"id": "ind-u6-33", "q": "What is a fulcrum security in a restructuring?", "a": "<p>The most senior security that is <b>not</b> repaid in full — the point in the capital structure where “value breaks”, and so the security most likely to be converted into the reorganised company’s <b>equity</b>. Classes above it are (roughly) paid in full; classes below it should get nothing.</p><p>Because it’s likely to become the new equity, its holders have the most leverage to shape the restructuring plan — which is why distressed investors focus on identifying it.</p>", "p": 235}, {"id": "ind-u6-34", "q": "What does the phrase \"debt to control\" mean?", "a": "<p>Buying a distressed company’s debt at a steep discount in order to <b>take control</b> of it through bankruptcy — when the debt is exchanged for new equity, the investor becomes the major (often controlling) shareholder.</p><p>It’s higher-risk and needs more capital than simply owning the fulcrum security, so it’s mostly done by large, multi-strategy firms investing across the capital structure (e.g. Apollo) rather than distressed hedge funds.</p>", "p": 235}, {"id": "ind-u6-35", "q": "What are “super-priority” claims?", "a": "<p>Secured <b>DIP</b> claims that rank above administrative claims and usually above pre-petition secured claims too. The money is lent once the company’s distress is recognised, and the court approves it only if it benefits stakeholders overall — which is why it can be repaid before even senior lenders.</p>", "p": 236}, {"id": "ind-u6-36", "q": "What does priming mean in restructuring financing?", "a": "<p>When a new tranche is placed <b>ahead</b> of existing lenders — including senior secured lenders — in the capital structure. In Chapter 11, DIP financing that primes existing liens gets a claim superior to everyone else’s.</p>", "p": 236}, {"id": "ind-u6-37", "q": "What is DIP financing and why is it considered as being safe for the lender?", "a": "<p>Debtor-in-possession financing is available only to companies that have already filed for bankruptcy. It sits at the very top of the capital structure with <b>super-priority</b> status, and often comes from the company’s existing lenders.</p><p>It’s considered very safe because it’s <b>secured</b> by collateral, has <b>super-priority</b>, and comes with very <b>restrictive covenants</b>.</p>", "p": 236}, {"id": "ind-u6-38", "q": "What conditions must be met for DIP financing to be approved?", "a": "<p>The court approves it only if pre-petition lenders are <b>adequately protected</b> by sufficient collateral. A DIP that primes existing secured creditors must leave them better off than in a liquidation. Once it’s shown not to unfairly impair secured holders and to benefit the company, the DIP funds operations through the reorganisation.</p>", "p": 236}, {"id": "ind-u6-39", "q": "Could you explain the purpose of the TWCF model in restructuring?", "a": "<p>The <b>13-week cash flow</b> model tracks the short-term liquidity of a company in or near distress, and is a standard requirement in a bankruptcy. Longer-term models are built too, but the priority is surviving the next few months.</p><p>It gives creditors transparency, helps management make critical decisions, and sizes <b>borrowing needs</b> during the reorganisation — if ending cash gets too low, the company must draw on its revolver or DIP.</p>", "p": 236}, {"id": "ind-u6-40", "q": "How does the TWCF model differ from typical financial models?", "a": "<ul><li><b>Cash basis, not accrual:</b> it tracks actual cash in and out (the direct method), because real cash is what determines liquidity.</li><li><b>Weekly rolling forecast:</b> updated every week, so projections can be compared with actuals and variances analysed to understand financial health and DIP needs.</li></ul>", "p": 236}]}, {"id": "ind-u6-l5", "title": "Claims & creditor protections", "pages": "237–238", "cards": [{"id": "ind-u6-41", "q": "Walk me through the basic structure of a TWCF model.", "a": "<ol><li><b>Operating receipts:</b> cash collected from customers.</li><li>Less <b>operating disbursements</b> — payroll, insurance, inventory purchases — to get operating cash flow.</li><li>Less <b>non-operating disbursements</b> — interest, principal amortisation, professional fees — to get <b>net cash flow</b> for the week.</li><li><b>Ending cash</b> = beginning cash + net cash flow (borrowing on the revolver/DIP if it falls too low).</li></ol>", "p": 237}, {"id": "ind-u6-42", "q": "What is an “amend and extend” agreement?", "a": "<p>The debtor and lenders agree to keep the debt in place but <b>push out the maturity</b>. In return, lenders usually get better terms — a higher interest rate, an amendment fee, a different amortisation schedule or other lender-friendly changes.</p>", "p": 237}, {"id": "ind-u6-43", "q": "How do you determine the value of a secured claim?", "a": "<p>A claim is secured only up to the value of its collateral:</p><div class=\"formula\">Secured claim = min(Claim amount, Collateral value)</div><p>Any shortfall — where collateral is worth less than the claim — becomes an <b>unsecured deficiency claim</b> treated as a general unsecured claim.</p>", "p": 237}, {"id": "ind-u6-44", "q": "The claim of a secured creditor was bifurcated into two claims. Could you explain when this might take place?", "a": "<p>When the collateral is worth <b>less</b> than the claim. The claim splits into a <b>secured</b> part (up to the collateral value) and an <b>unsecured deficiency</b> part, treated like any other unsecured claim. E.g. a $100m claim backed by $70m of collateral becomes a $70m secured claim and a $30m unsecured claim.</p>", "p": 237}, {"id": "ind-u6-45", "q": "How does the fiduciary duty of management change during a bankruptcy?", "a": "<p>The book’s view: once a company is insolvent or close to it (the “zone of insolvency”), management’s fiduciary duties shift from equity holders towards <b>creditors</b>, since creditors are now the residual owners of the company’s value.</p><p class=\"muted\">Under Delaware law, the court said in 2007 (Gheewalla) that duties don’t formally shift in the zone of insolvency; creditors only gain standing to sue for breaches once the company is actually insolvent. In practice, boards must weigh creditors’ interests as distress deepens.</p>", "p": 237}, {"id": "ind-u6-46", "q": "What does a negative pledge provision restrict a borrower from doing?", "a": "<p>The borrower agrees <b>not to pledge</b> specified assets as collateral to other lenders. That protects the existing lender from its available collateral shrinking or a new creditor jumping ahead of it — and makes it harder for the borrower to raise more secured debt.</p>", "p": 237}, {"id": "ind-u6-47", "q": "What is the purpose of adequate protection?", "a": "<p>It ensures secured creditors’ interest in their collateral is protected — not sold off, used up or damaged — while the debtor uses it during bankruptcy. As long as secured creditors are adequately protected, the debtor can use cash collateral and other liened assets. Protection can include cash payments or replacement liens, which get priority status. Without it, creditors can seek relief from the automatic stay and take the collateral.</p>", "p": 237}, {"id": "ind-u6-48", "q": "What is the main difference between liquidation done under Chapter 7 and 11?", "a": "<p>Liquidation can happen under either. Under <b>Chapter 7</b>, management is removed and a <b>trustee</b> runs the liquidation. Under Chapter 11, the debtor (management) usually stays in control and runs the sale process under a liquidating plan.</p>", "p": 238}, {"id": "ind-u6-49", "q": "What is a plan support agreement?", "a": "<p>An agreement (often called a restructuring support agreement) in which signing creditors commit to vote for a plan matching its terms. It cuts the cost and length of the bankruptcy and reduces disruption for the business, employees and customers.</p>", "p": 238}, {"id": "ind-u6-50", "q": "What is the difference between consent and a waiver?", "a": "<p>Both are creditors allowing the company to breach a covenant. A <b>waiver</b> is temporary (e.g. for a one-off breach from an unforeseen event); <b>consent</b> (an amendment) is permanent.</p>", "p": 238}]}, {"id": "ind-u6-l6", "title": "363 sales, committees & bankruptcy law", "pages": "238–239", "cards": [{"id": "ind-u6-51", "q": "What are debentures?", "a": "<p><b>Unsecured</b> bonds, with covenants much less restrictive than bank loans. In bankruptcy, holders have no claim on specific assets and are paid only after secured creditors — if anything is left.</p><p class=\"muted\">In the UK and elsewhere, “debenture” can mean a secured instrument; the book uses the US meaning.</p>", "p": 238}, {"id": "ind-u6-52", "q": "What does a sale under Section 363 of the US Bankruptcy Code involve?", "a": "<p>A court-supervised sale of some or all of a debtor’s assets during bankruptcy, often letting buyers pick up assets at a bargain. An initial bidder — the <b>stalking horse</b> — sets the floor price, and other parties can then top it within a set period in an auction. The sale needs Bankruptcy Court approval; the big risk is a poorly run process that the court won’t approve.</p>", "p": 238}, {"id": "ind-u6-53", "q": "Would a Section 363 Sale hurt creditors because of the loss of collateral?", "a": "<p>Generally no. Creditors are the key stakeholders the sale should benefit, and they can object — e.g. if assets are being sold far below fair value — and the court weighs their objections before approving. A completed sale usually means no objection prevailed.</p><p>363 sales also let secured creditors <b>credit bid</b>: bid using the debt owed to them (secured by that asset) instead of cash, cancelling that debt in exchange for the asset.</p>", "p": 238}, {"id": "ind-u6-54", "q": "What is credit bidding?", "a": "<p>In a bankruptcy sale, a secured lender can bid for its collateral using its <b>claim</b> as currency instead of new cash (only secured claims can credit bid). Investors — often financial sponsors — can buy secured debt from existing creditors at a discount and then credit bid the <b>full face value</b> of the claim to acquire the underlying assets.</p>", "p": 238}, {"id": "ind-u6-55", "q": "What is the purpose of bondholder committees?", "a": "<p>In an out-of-court restructuring, bondholders often band together into a committee (ad hoc group) to negotiate with the company with more leverage, raising the chance the company accepts their terms. Committees often hire a restructuring advisor to support their analysis and position.</p>", "p": 239}, {"id": "ind-u6-56", "q": "Why might certain distressed funds be hesitant to join a bondholder committee?", "a": "<p><b>Disclosure and trading restrictions.</b> Committee members typically must disclose their holdings and when they buy or sell, and may receive private information that restricts trading. Many distressed funds prefer to stay free to trade without disclosing their positions.</p>", "p": 239}, {"id": "ind-u6-57", "q": "Why does the holdout problem occur in out-of-court restructurings?", "a": "<p>Out of court, a company can’t force individual creditors to accept less — each must agree, and consensus gets harder with more parties. A bondholder may reason that if enough others accept a haircut, it’s better off refusing and insisting on full recovery. In court, by contrast, the agreed recovery binds the <b>entire class</b>.</p>", "p": 239}, {"id": "ind-u6-58", "q": "What are administrative claims?", "a": "<p>Post-petition claims for the costs of keeping the business running and administering the bankruptcy — e.g. post-petition wages, goods and services received after filing, and professional fees (legal, advisory, turnaround consultants). They rank ahead of pre-petition unsecured claims.</p>", "p": 239}, {"id": "ind-u6-59", "q": "Name some specific add-backs to EBITDA in the context of restructuring.", "a": "<ul><li>Professional fees (Rx advisory, accounting, legal, turnaround consultants)</li><li>Severance costs</li><li>Legal settlements</li><li>Inventory or PP&amp;E write-downs</li><li>Goodwill impairments</li><li>Uncollectible receivables (bad A/R)</li><li>Gains/losses on asset sales and facility closures</li></ul>", "p": 239}]}, {"id": "ind-u6-l7", "title": "Bankruptcy legal concepts", "pages": "239–240", "cards": [{"id": "ind-u6-60", "q": "What is a critical vendor motion?", "a": "<p>A court-approved request to pay certain vendors’ <b>pre-petition</b> claims because the business can’t operate without them. The reasoning: not paying would destroy value and hurt even the higher-priority creditors.</p>", "p": 239}, {"id": "ind-u6-61", "q": "What is a fraudulent conveyance?", "a": "<p>A transfer of cash, property or other assets that defrauds creditors — made with intent to hinder or delay them, or (constructively) for less than fair value while the company was insolvent. Creditors challenging it must show the company was insolvent at the time (for constructive fraud) or that the transfer was meant to avoid its obligations. If they win, the transfer can be unwound and proceeds <b>clawed back</b>. Failed LBOs are a common source of these claims.</p>", "p": 239}, {"id": "ind-u6-62", "q": "Give me an example of a spring forward provision.", "a": "<p>A term loan that matures <b>after</b> a more junior senior note might include a “springing maturity”: if a trigger occurs, the loan’s maturity jumps forward — usually to about <b>90 days before</b> the note’s maturity. Triggers can include the note not being refinanced by a set date, heavy revolver usage, a ratings downgrade, a failed exchange offer, or breaching a financial covenant like a leverage ratio. It stops junior debt being repaid before the senior loan.</p>", "p": 240}, {"id": "ind-u6-63", "q": "What are voidable preferences?", "a": "<p>Payments or asset transfers to a creditor within ~<b>90 days</b> before filing (one year for insiders) that favour that creditor over others — e.g. paying a junior creditor right before bankruptcy, jumping the waterfall. If proven, the payment is voided and must be returned to the estate for distribution to the rightful creditors.</p>", "p": 240}, {"id": "ind-u6-64", "q": "What does a cramdown refer to in a restructuring scenario?", "a": "<p>The court confirms a POR and imposes it on an impaired class that <b>voted against</b> it. The court can do so despite objections, as long as the plan meets the fairness requirements (e.g. it doesn’t discriminate unfairly and is fair and equitable to the dissenting class).</p>", "p": 240}, {"id": "ind-u6-65", "q": "What is an equitable subordination?", "a": "<p>A rare but serious risk for secured creditors: the court can push a creditor’s claim <b>down</b> the priority order (even equalise it with others) if that creditor engaged in misconduct or acted in bad faith — e.g. an insider using its position to manipulate the debt to the detriment of other creditors.</p>", "p": 240}, {"id": "ind-u6-66", "q": "How is a capitalization table used for restructuring purposes?", "a": "<p>The Rx banker builds and keeps updating a cap table listing every claim on the company, focusing on its debt. For each tranche: maturity, coupon/pricing, credit rating and market price (if traded), plus key credit metrics like leverage and interest coverage. Mapping upcoming maturities and interest dates shows how urgent the liquidity situation is and whether DIP financing is needed.</p>", "p": 240}, {"id": "ind-u6-67", "q": "Do equity shareholders receive any form of compensation during Chapter 11s?", "a": "<p>In theory, no — preferred and common equity sit at the bottom and are usually wiped out. Occasionally, though, old equity gets a small <b>“tip”</b> — around 1–2% of the reorganised company’s equity or a nominal payment — typically to avoid objections and speed up the process.</p>", "p": 240}]}], "ind-u7": [{"id": "ind-u7-l1", "title": "REITs: financials & valuation", "pages": "241–243", "cards": [{"id": "ind-u7-1", "q": "Do REITs pay taxes?", "a": "<p>Not at the corporate level. REITs are <b>pass-throughs</b>: profits aren’t taxed at the company, and shareholders pay tax on the dividends instead. To qualify, a REIT must distribute at least <b>90%</b> of its taxable income as dividends, so it can’t hoard profits like a C-corp.</p><p>REIT dividends are mostly <b>non-qualified</b>, taxed at ordinary income rates. C-corps face double taxation (corporate tax, then tax on dividends), but their dividends are qualified and taxed at lower rates.</p><p>REITs still pay <b>property taxes</b>, which sit in direct real estate expenses on the income statement.</p>", "p": 241}, {"id": "ind-u7-2", "q": "Walk me through the income statement of a REIT.", "a": "<ol><li><b>Revenue:</b> mainly rental income, plus ancillary income such as management fees.</li><li>Less <b>direct property expenses</b> — utilities, marketing, payroll, maintenance, property taxes — gives <b>NOI</b>, property-level profitability.</li><li>Less <b>corporate overhead/SG&amp;A</b> and <b>depreciation</b> (large under US GAAP given the assets; none under IFRS) gives operating profit.</li><li>Less <b>interest expense</b> — usually large, since REITs borrow heavily.</li><li>REITs constantly buy and sell properties, so there are often significant <b>gains/losses on sales</b> and discontinued operations.</li></ol>", "p": 241}, {"id": "ind-u7-3", "q": "What is NOI and why is it an important metric?", "a": "<div class=\"formula\">NOI = Rental income − Property operating expenses</div><p>Property-level expenses include utilities, marketing, payroll, maintenance and property taxes. NOI is profit <b>before</b> depreciation, corporate SG&amp;A and interest.</p><p>It isolates property-level profitability, so you can compare properties and portfolios without the noise of corporate costs or leverage — the closest thing in REIT financials to gross profit. It’s also the numerator of the <b>cap rate</b>.</p>", "p": 241}, {"id": "ind-u7-4", "q": "Define funds from operations (FFO) and why is it important?", "a": "<p>FFO is the standard REIT operating performance measure. Under US GAAP:</p><div class=\"formula\">FFO = Net income + Real estate D&amp;A − Gains on property sales (+ impairments, with NCI adjustments)</div><p>It adds back depreciation (real estate tends to hold its value) and strips out one-off sale gains. Like cash from operations, it ignores working capital changes. Think of it as roughly <b>levered EBITDA</b>: unlike EBITDA, it’s after interest and other non-common-equity claims (debt, preferred, non-controlling interests).</p>", "p": 242}, {"id": "ind-u7-5", "q": "For REITs valuing properties, how do regulations differ between GAAP and IFRS?", "a": "<p>Under <b>IFRS</b>, REITs can carry properties as “investment properties” at fair value: no depreciation, and properties are regularly <b>marked to market</b>, with revaluation gains running through the income statement.</p><p>So the IFRS net income → FFO bridge needs no depreciation add-back, but you must <b>subtract</b> revaluation gains. Under <b>US GAAP</b>, properties sit at historical cost and are depreciated.</p>", "p": 242}, {"id": "ind-u7-6", "q": "What is the difference between FFO and AFFO?", "a": "<p><b>AFFO</b> (adjusted FFO, or cash available for distribution) subtracts recurring <b>capex</b> needed to maintain the properties (and adjusts for non-recurring items and non-cash items like straight-line rent), getting closer to free cash flow — though still without working capital changes.</p><div class=\"formula\">AFFO ≈ FFO + Non-recurring expenses − Maintenance capex</div>", "p": 242}, {"id": "ind-u7-7", "q": "Walk me through a NAV valuation.", "a": "<p>The most common REIT valuation — essentially rebuilding the balance sheet at market values:</p><ol><li><b>Value NOI-generating real estate:</b> forward NOI ÷ cap rate. The cap rate is the most important assumption; use separate NOIs and cap rates by property, region or type if available.</li><li><b>Value other income separately:</b> ancillary/management fee income and JV income, each capitalised at an appropriate cap rate.</li><li><b>Subtract</b> the capitalised value of corporate overhead and capex needed to sustain the properties that isn’t in NOI.</li><li><b>Add other assets</b> not yet counted — cash, construction in progress (at book or a premium).</li><li><b>Subtract debt</b> and debt-like claims (usually at book, unless market values differ meaningfully).</li></ol><p>The result is <b>NAV-derived equity value</b> — compare with market cap, or divide by shares to compare with the share price.</p>", "p": 242}, {"id": "ind-u7-8", "q": "Why might the NAV be preferable to a DCF for REITs?", "a": "<p>REIT balance sheets are full of real estate with <b>observable market values</b> from comparable assets. NAV simply restates the balance sheet at fair value using cap rates. Most industries can’t do that, which is why they rely on DCFs instead.</p>", "p": 243}, {"id": "ind-u7-9", "q": "What is a cap rate, and why is it important?", "a": "<p>The most common real estate valuation metric, comparing unlevered profit to property value:</p><div class=\"formula\">Cap rate = NOI ÷ Property value</div><p>A $2m property with $100k of NOI has a 5.0% cap rate. It’s an inverted multiple — a <b>higher</b> cap rate means a <b>lower</b> valuation. A property with a higher cap rate than comparable properties is either undervalued or has higher risk, lower growth or lower expected returns.</p>", "p": 243}, {"id": "ind-u7-10", "q": "What are the most common methods for valuing REITs?", "a": "<ul><li><b>NAV:</b> for US REITs, whose book values aren’t marked to market.</li><li><b>P/B:</b> for IFRS REITs, whose book values already reflect market value.</li><li><b>Comps</b>, using <b>P/FFO</b> (equity value ÷ FFO) and <b>P/AFFO</b> (equity value ÷ AFFO).</li><li><b>Intrinsic:</b> the <b>DDM</b> works well because REITs must pay out at least 90% of taxable income; a DCF is possible but used far less than NAV.</li></ul>", "p": 243}, {"id": "ind-u7-11", "q": "Why is the self-storage industry known to be such a resilient performer?", "a": "<p>Self-storage is one of real estate’s most reliable, non-cyclical asset classes. In 2008, self-storage REITs were the only real estate sector with positive returns, while hospitality fell 50%+.</p><p>Demand has been driven by urbanisation and millennials renting rather than buying homes. Storage units have become an extension of the home — extra closet or garage space — not just a place for unwanted items.</p>", "p": 243}]}], "ind-u8": [{"id": "ind-u8-l1", "title": "Real estate PE: valuation & returns", "pages": "244–245", "cards": [{"id": "ind-u8-1", "q": "What are the three methods for valuing real estate assets?", "a": "<ol><li><b>Income approach (direct capitalisation):</b> <div class=\"formula\">Property value = NOI ÷ Market cap rate</div></li><li><b>Sales comparison:</b> recent similar transactions give price per unit or per square foot (and current market cap rates).</li><li><b>Replacement cost:</b> an investor shouldn’t pay more for a property than it would cost to build it.</li></ol>", "p": 244}, {"id": "ind-u8-2", "q": "Compare the cap rates and risk profiles for each of the main property types.", "a": "<ul><li><b>Hotels:</b> the <b>highest</b> cap rates — cash flow depends on nightly stays (extremely short “leases”) plus operationally intensive restaurants and conferences.</li><li><b>Retail:</b> tenant creditworthiness is increasingly in doubt because of e-commerce.</li><li><b>Office:</b> closely tied to the economy, but with longer leases.</li><li><b>Industrial:</b> benefits from e-commerce demand and long leases.</li><li><b>Multifamily:</b> seen as the <b>safest</b> — people need somewhere to live whatever the economy does — so typically the lowest cap rates.</li></ul><p class=\"muted\">Since the book, office has been hit by remote work and its risk premium has risen sharply.</p>", "p": 244}, {"id": "ind-u8-3", "q": "Walk me through a basic cash flow pro forma for a real estate asset.", "a": "<ol><li><b>Revenue:</b> mainly rental income plus other income, less vacancy and leasing incentives (rent abatements, concessions).</li><li>Less <b>operating expenses</b> → <b>NOI</b>.</li><li>Less <b>capex</b>, and include the purchase price and sale proceeds → <b>unlevered cash flow</b>.</li><li>Less <b>financing costs</b> (interest, principal, plus loan proceeds/repayment) → <b>levered cash flow</b>.</li></ol>", "p": 244}, {"id": "ind-u8-4", "q": "Describe the four main real estate investment strategies.", "a": "<ol><li><b>Core:</b> lowest risk and return — newer properties in great locations, high occupancy, very creditworthy tenants.</li><li><b>Core-plus:</b> slightly riskier — some leasing upside or minor capital improvements. What most people picture as real estate investing.</li><li><b>Value-add:</b> meaningfully riskier — significant lease-up, older buildings needing real capex, secondary/tertiary locations or weaker tenants.</li><li><b>Opportunistic:</b> highest risk and target returns — ground-up development or redevelopment.</li></ol>", "p": 244}, {"id": "ind-u8-5", "q": "If I paid $100 million for a building and it has 75% leverage, how much does it need to sell for to double my equity?", "a": "<p><b>$125 million.</b> 75% leverage means $75m of debt and $25m of equity. Doubling equity means $50m back to you, and you still need to repay the $75m of debt: $50m + $75m = $125m (ignoring interim cash flow and costs).</p>", "p": 244}, {"id": "ind-u8-6", "q": "If you had two identical buildings in the same condition and right next to each other, what factors would you look at to determine which property is more valuable?", "a": "<p>With location and quality equal, focus on the <b>cash flows</b>:</p><ul><li><b>How much:</b> average rents and occupancy — management and leasing can differ even between identical buildings.</li><li><b>How risky:</b> the rent roll — tenants’ creditworthiness and lease lengths.</li></ul><p>Then tie it together with <b>value = NOI ÷ cap rate</b>: the cash flow amount drives NOI, and their riskiness drives the cap rate. Higher, safer cash flow means higher value.</p>", "p": 245}, {"id": "ind-u8-7", "q": "Assume you purchased a property for $1 million at a 7.5% cap rate, had 0% NOI growth throughout the holding period, and exited at the same cap rate after three years. What is your IRR?", "a": "<p><b>7.5%.</b> With unchanged NOI and the same exit cap rate, the property sells for the same $1m — no gain on sale. The only return is the annual NOI of $75k ($1m × 7.5%), so the unlevered IRR equals the cap rate.</p>", "p": 245}, {"id": "ind-u8-8", "q": "If you purchase a property for $1 million at a 5.0% cap rate with 60% leverage and a 5.0% fixed cost of debt, what is the cash-on-cash yield?", "a": "<div class=\"formula\">Cash-on-cash = Levered cash flow ÷ Equity invested</div><ul><li>NOI = $1m × 5.0% = $50k</li><li>Debt = $600k; equity = $400k</li><li>Interest = $600k × 5.0% = $30k</li><li>Levered cash flow = $50k − $30k = $20k</li></ul><p>Cash-on-cash = $20k ÷ $400k = <b>5.0%</b>. Since the cost of debt equals the cap rate, leverage neither helps nor hurts the yield.</p>", "p": 245}, {"id": "ind-u8-9", "q": "Do you have any questions for me? (REPE Context)", "a": "<p><b>Role:</b></p><ul><li>Is there a strict split between acquisitions and asset management?</li><li>How much exposure do juniors get to the legal side of closing deals?</li><li>How much travel is there for junior team members?</li></ul><p><b>Strategy:</b></p><ul><li>Does the firm focus on one risk profile (core to opportunistic) or several?</li><li>Equity only, or debt and equity?</li><li>Development, or only acquisitions?</li></ul>", "p": 245}, {"id": "ind-u8-10", "q": "Walk me through a back-of-the-envelope multifamily real estate acquisition model.", "a": "<p>Usually a separate ~10-minute test on paper or in Excel. A simple structure:</p><ol><li><b>Purchase price</b> and <b>sources &amp; uses</b> — debt (LTV × price) and equity.</li><li><b>Revenue:</b> units × average monthly rent × 12, less vacancy; grow it annually.</li><li>Less <b>operating expenses</b> (often % of revenue or $ per unit) → <b>NOI</b>.</li><li>Less <b>capex/reserves</b> and <b>debt service</b> → levered cash flow.</li><li><b>Exit:</b> final-year (or forward) NOI ÷ exit cap rate, less selling costs and loan repayment.</li><li><b>Returns:</b> levered IRR and equity multiple, plus cash-on-cash each year.</li></ol>", "p": 245}]}], "ind-u9": [{"id": "ind-u9-l1", "title": "Industrials: trends, M&A & valuation", "pages": "246–248", "cards": [{"id": "ind-u9-1", "q": "Why have industrial manufactures shifted their focus towards the aftermarket?", "a": "<p>Over the past decade, manufacturers have become more service-oriented, growing <b>aftermarket</b> revenue — maintenance, spare parts and other value-added services.</p><ul><li><b>Less cyclicality:</b> new equipment demand is cyclical and markets are mature, so reducing reliance on new sales lowers risk.</li><li><b>Cheaper growth:</b> organic growth from new products is costly and capital-intensive; aftermarket revenue is predictable and recurring.</li><li><b>Proven resilience:</b> in the last recession, companies with more service revenue held up far better than those 90%+ dependent on new equipment sales.</li></ul><p>Some built service businesses in-house, but most expanded through <b>M&amp;A</b> — a trend accelerated by COVID, as demand for new equipment fell.</p>", "p": 246}, {"id": "ind-u9-2", "q": "What is the trend of servitization in manufacturing?", "a": "<p>Manufacturers adding <b>service revenue streams</b> themselves — spare parts, repairs, maintenance, overhauls, performance monitoring, customer support — instead of leaving them to third parties.</p><p>Previously they just built equipment and handed sales, distribution and support to outside vendors and specialists. Now they sell “systems” focused on the outcome their products deliver, servicing them directly to ensure satisfaction — improving retention and margins.</p>", "p": 246}, {"id": "ind-u9-3", "q": "Why is there such a high level of M&A activity in the industrial manufacturing space?", "a": "<p>The industry is made up of <b>hundreds of specialised niches</b>, each with differentiated capabilities, and product innovation is constant. Incumbents would rather buy companies with proven technology and existing customers than spend heavily on R&amp;D.</p><p>Financial sponsors are very active: they buy niche players, grow them through add-ons, then sell to large strategics like Honeywell, Siemens, ABB, ASSA ABLOY and Johnson Controls — for whom recent growth has been almost entirely <b>inorganic</b>. Sponsors invest knowing there should be strategic buyers at exit. In general, the more specialised niches an industry has, the more M&amp;A it sees.</p>", "p": 246}, {"id": "ind-u9-4", "q": "Which valuation multiples are most often used to value companies in industrials?", "a": "<ul><li><b>EV/EBITDA</b> — the most common, especially for industrial tech.</li><li><b>EV/(EBITDA − Capex)</b> — for capital-intensive manufacturers.</li><li><b>EV/EBITDAR</b> — for transport and logistics companies that lease equipment such as trucks.</li><li>Transport-specific: EV/freight tonnage or volume, EV/miles driven, EV/revenue per mile or per weight unit.</li></ul>", "p": 246}, {"id": "ind-u9-5", "q": "Let’s say you're looking at an industrials company. What business and industry characteristics would you first consider?", "a": "<ul><li><b>End markets:</b> which customers it serves and how diversified they are drives how cyclical revenue is; how essential the products are and the strength of customer relationships drive how recurring it is.</li><li><b>Supply chain:</b> how many suppliers it depends on and how integrated it is; how broad its distribution network is and room to expand into new channels.</li><li><b>Product value and upgrade cycles:</b> how valuable the offering is and how often it needs upgrades or recurring services like maintenance.</li><li><b>Competitive landscape:</b> barriers to entry and how its products stack up — which drive revenue stability and pricing power.</li></ul>", "p": 247}, {"id": "ind-u9-6", "q": "An industrials company has shown COVID-related impaired revenue. What metric would you look at to assess the damage and predict the pace of recovery?", "a": "<p><b>Backlog</b> — contracts secured but not yet earned. Ideally backlog <b>grew</b> even as revenue fell, because customers postponed projects (by choice or due to restrictions) rather than cancelling them. In H1 2020, big industrial conglomerates highlighted healthy backlogs on earnings calls as evidence of a swift recovery ahead.</p><p>A shrinking or lagging backlog vs peers is a bad sign: the company failed to win new commitments during lockdowns, or customers cancelled projects or went bankrupt.</p>", "p": 247}, {"id": "ind-u9-7", "q": "Walk me through the stages from Industry 1.0 to Industry 4.0.", "a": "<ol><li><b>Industry 1.0 — the Industrial Revolution:</b> manufacturing moved from human labour to machines powered by water, steam and coal.</li><li><b>Industry 2.0 — the Technological Revolution:</b> electricity enabled mass production and more sophisticated machines.</li><li><b>Industry 3.0 — the Digital Revolution:</b> early (huge, low-powered) computers laid the groundwork for electronics and IT infrastructure.</li><li><b>Industry 4.0 — the Automation Revolution:</b> where we are now. Machines can make decisions and govern themselves using IoT, cloud computing and big data — smart factories, sensors, voice and facial recognition.</li></ol>", "p": 247}, {"id": "ind-u9-8", "q": "How did Industry 4.0 help bridge the gap between OEMs and their customers?", "a": "<p>Before, the OEM made replacement parts and third-party service contractors did the planned maintenance and repairs, so OEMs had little visibility into their installed base and little customer contact.</p><p><b>Industrial IoT</b> lets OEMs monitor performance and manage uptime across the whole asset life cycle, and offer guaranteed <b>service level agreements (SLAs)</b> directly — building much closer customer relationships.</p>", "p": 248}, {"id": "ind-u9-9", "q": "What are some concerns surrounding Industry 4.0 that exist to this day?", "a": "<ul><li><b>Machine-to-machine trust:</b> M2M/IoT communication still isn’t fully reliable or stable enough to trust completely.</li><li><b>Data security:</b> the volume of data and level of system access raise IT security risks, especially at facilities with outdated security.</li><li><b>Costly mistakes:</b> small errors in manufacturing can be very expensive and disrupt production, so new processes are rolled out gradually at low volumes.</li></ul>", "p": 248}, {"id": "ind-u9-10", "q": "What is Industry 5.0, and what will it do for manufacturing once this next phase arrives?", "a": "<p><b>Humans working alongside robots and smart machines.</b> Where Industry 4.0 is about automation, 5.0 is about collaboration — using IoT, big data, AI and ML for repetitive tasks while humans make the perception-driven design and customisation decisions. It’s driven by consumer demand for customised products, which needs human creativity.</p>", "p": 248}]}, {"id": "ind-u9-l2", "title": "Manufacturing, maintenance & transport", "pages": "248–250", "cards": [{"id": "ind-u9-11", "q": "What are co-bots, and what makes them unique?", "a": "<p><b>Collaborative robots</b>, designed to work safely right next to humans — rather than robots working on their own while people supervise from a safe distance.</p>", "p": 248}, {"id": "ind-u9-12", "q": "What is the difference between customer support agreements and outcome contracts?", "a": "<p><b>Customer support agreement:</b> sets out the work to be done, the terms and the price of each service.</p><p><b>Outcome contract:</b> pricing and terms are tied to hitting specific, measurable performance metrics (KPI-based) — the provider is paid for results, e.g. equipment uptime.</p>", "p": 248}, {"id": "ind-u9-13", "q": "What is the difference between process manufacturing and discrete manufacturing?", "a": "<p><b>Process manufacturing:</b> follows a formula or recipe, blending ingredients and raw materials — food, beverages, chemicals, pharmaceuticals. The output can’t be broken back down into its inputs.</p><p><b>Discrete manufacturing:</b> produces distinct, countable items assembled from identifiable parts — often lower volume and higher complexity (e.g. cars, machinery, or components like nuts and bolts).</p>", "p": 248}, {"id": "ind-u9-14", "q": "Tell me about the differences between predictive, prescriptive, and condition-based maintenance.", "a": "<p><b>Predictive:</b> uses vibration, temperature or pressure data to spot degradation and fix it <b>before</b> a failure.</p><p><b>Prescriptive:</b> adds ML/AI on top of predictive maintenance to improve accuracy and recommend specific corrective actions, not just flag a likely failure.</p><p><b>Condition-based:</b> maintenance is triggered when a preset condition or threshold is hit (e.g. smoke, unstable temperatures, slower cooldowns) — a rule rather than a forecast, so some deterioration may already have started.</p>", "p": 249}, {"id": "ind-u9-15", "q": "What are some basic unit economics for a truck transportation company you would look at to forecast revenue?", "a": "<p>Start with historical hauls per year, average haul length in miles and total shipping revenue to back into <b>revenue per mile</b>.</p><div class=\"formula\">Revenue = Hauls × Average miles per haul × Revenue per mile</div><p>Build it out with the number of repeat clients, the current pipeline of orders and expected new contract wins. Alternatively, forecast per driver, per truck or per project.</p>", "p": 249}, {"id": "ind-u9-16", "q": "What are some of the cost drivers you would look at for a truck transportation company?", "a": "<ol><li><b>Drivers:</b> the biggest cost — number of drivers and pay (usually per mile or hourly rather than salary).</li><li><b>Fleet capex:</b> number of trucks, average fleet age, useful life and annual repair costs.</li><li><b>Others:</b> fuel, insurance, S&amp;M and rent — insurance, S&amp;M and rent matter much less than the first two.</li></ol>", "p": 249}, {"id": "ind-u9-17", "q": "Would you consider distribution and transportation service providers to be a low-margin business?", "a": "<p>Historically, yes — they’ve sat near the bottom on margins. But technology offers ways to differentiate and charge more:</p><ul><li><b>Route optimisation</b> using geographic information systems (GIS) makes each trip more efficient.</li><li><b>Asset location tracking</b> (barcode/QR scanning) gives real-time visibility of parts and inventory, improving data accuracy and helping with compliance (e.g. for pharmaceuticals).</li></ul>", "p": 249}, {"id": "ind-u9-18", "q": "Why does the airline industry have such low margins?", "a": "<p>A heavy <b>fixed-cost</b> structure — buying fleets is very expensive, and maintenance, repairs and the airport footprint add more. On top of that come high variable costs (pilots, crews, ground staff, security).</p><p>Airlines also struggle to cut costs because so much spending is safety-critical: skimping on repairs or security, or cutting flights, risks severe reputational damage. Add intense competition, fuel price volatility and sensitivity to shocks (like COVID), and margins stay razor-thin.</p>", "p": 250}, {"id": "ind-u9-19", "q": "What is the difference between fully autonomous vehicles and self-driving cars?", "a": "<p>Often used interchangeably, but: a <b>fully autonomous</b> vehicle makes all its own decisions with no human supervision (Level 5). A <b>self-driving</b> car can drive itself in some conditions, but a human must be ready to take over (around Level 3).</p>", "p": 250}, {"id": "ind-u9-20", "q": "Why has the rollout of Waymo and self-driving been done at such a slow pace?", "a": "<p>Despite its head start, Waymo faced repeated delays and revised timelines. With heavy regulation, <b>safety</b> is the top priority — a single fatal accident could halt the programme and destroy public trust in the technology.</p><p class=\"muted\">Since the book, Waymo has launched fully driverless paid rides in several US cities.</p>", "p": 250}]}, {"id": "ind-u9-l3", "title": "Smart buildings, pumps & materials", "pages": "250–253", "cards": [{"id": "ind-u9-21", "q": "What does energy management software (EMS) refer to, and what role does it play in increasing the number of so-called \"smart buildings\"?", "a": "<p>Software that tracks energy use, identifies waste and leaks, and is the core technology behind <b>smart buildings</b>. It lets owners optimise HVAC, lighting and energy use using simulations and demand analysis — saving energy, cutting utility bills and helping meet local sustainability rules.</p>", "p": 250}, {"id": "ind-u9-22", "q": "What does industrial access control involve, and what value does it provide to end-users?", "a": "<p>Systems that control who can enter or leave specific areas of a facility and block unauthorised access — electronic access devices, electronic and mechanical locks, and integrated surveillance. They protect physical assets (expensive equipment, hazardous areas) as well as intangibles like documents and data.</p>", "p": 250}, {"id": "ind-u9-23", "q": "What is the difference between positive displacement pumps and centrifugal pumps?", "a": "<p><b>Positive displacement:</b> traps a fixed volume of fluid and forces it into the discharge (rotary, reciprocating or diaphragm designs). The key trait: <b>flow rate stays roughly constant</b> even as pressure changes.</p><p><b>Centrifugal:</b> a spinning impeller, driven by a motor, adds velocity to the fluid, which is converted into outlet pressure. Flow rate <b>varies</b> with pressure.</p>", "p": 251}, {"id": "ind-u9-24", "q": "What are the two most common types of positive displacement pumps?", "a": "<p>Rotary and <b>reciprocating</b>. Reciprocating pumps work by expanding and contracting a chamber:</p><ul><li><b>Diaphragm pump:</b> a flexing diaphragm draws fluid in as the chamber expands and pushes it out as it contracts.</li><li><b>Piston pump:</b> a piston’s stroke draws fluid in, and the reverse stroke forces it out under pressure.</li><li><b>Plunger pump:</b> like a piston pump, but a smooth plunger slides through a stationary high-pressure seal — better for higher pressures.</li></ul>", "p": 251}, {"id": "ind-u9-25", "q": "List a few manufacturing KPIs you would look at to measure the efficiency of a factory.", "a": "<ul><li><b>Throughput</b> = units produced ÷ time — production capacity of a machine, line or plant.</li><li><b>On-time delivery</b> = orders delivered on schedule ÷ orders due.</li><li><b>Capacity utilisation</b> = actual output ÷ total capacity — the aim is close to 100%.</li><li><b>First pass yield</b> = units made right first time ÷ total units — with no rework.</li><li><b>Machine downtime rate</b> = downtime hours ÷ (downtime + operating hours).</li></ul>", "p": 252}, {"id": "ind-u9-26", "q": "What are some ongoing trends in the plastics industry?", "a": "<ul><li><b>ESG:</b> OEMs and suppliers, by choice or regulation, are committing to zero-waste initiatives and recycled materials.</li><li><b>Biodegradable plastics:</b> e.g. bioresins made from plant-based, more compostable materials.</li><li><b>Pro-degradant additives:</b> making conventional plastics (like durable straws) break down faster.</li></ul>", "p": 252}, {"id": "ind-u9-27", "q": "What is reinforced plastic, and why are they increasingly being used?", "a": "<p>Plastics reinforced with fibres such as carbon, glass, metal or graphite — an alternative to metal. Long used mainly in autos, they’re spreading into medical, defence and construction because they’re lightweight yet durable and flexible to design with.</p>", "p": 252}, {"id": "ind-u9-28", "q": "What impact will the trend of electric vehicles (EVs) have on the polymer market?", "a": "<p><b>Positive.</b> Automakers prefer polymers to metal for lighter, more energy-efficient EVs, and polymers are easier to mould and more versatile for engineers. Advanced polymers like polyphthalamide (PPA) and polyamide (PA) are expected to be favoured because they withstand the higher temperatures in EV systems.</p>", "p": 253}, {"id": "ind-u9-29", "q": "What is additive manufacturing, and what benefits does it provide?", "a": "<p><b>3D printing</b>: building parts layer by layer from CAD designs or 3D scans. It creates light but durable, precise, customised parts quickly, without sacrificing quality. Its main use so far is <b>rapid prototyping</b> — short turnaround (~1–2 weeks) but only low-volume runs, mostly small plastic parts.</p>", "p": 253}, {"id": "ind-u9-30", "q": "What are the differences between injection molding and urethane casting?", "a": "<p><b>Injection moulding:</b> molten plastic is injected into a durable mould, then cooled. Expensive tooling, but handles high temperatures and thousands of cycles a day — ideal for <b>low-cost mass production</b>.</p><p><b>Urethane casting:</b> liquid silicone is poured around a master pattern to make a mould, then urethane is cast in it. The silicone mould is quick and cheap to make but wears out fast — suited to <b>prototypes and small runs</b>.</p>", "p": 253}]}, {"id": "ind-u9-l4", "title": "Shipping companies", "pages": "253–255", "cards": [{"id": "ind-u9-31", "q": "How does a maritime/shipping company generate revenue?", "a": "<p>Shipping companies own or operate vessels carrying commodities (crude and refined oil, coal, iron ore) and traded goods, earning daily rates from charterers like oil majors, miners and goods suppliers.</p><ul><li><b>Time charter:</b> a fixed daily rate for a set period, usually 1–3 years — predictable revenue. The charterer usually pays voyage expenses (port and fuel).</li><li><b>Bareboat charter:</b> a long-term lease (often 5+ years) of an empty vessel; the charterer acts almost like an owner, covering crew and operating costs, while the owner bears only capital costs.</li><li><b>Spot (voyage) charter:</b> a rate for a specific voyage from loading to discharge port, based on current market rates — much shorter and more volatile. The owner usually covers voyage expenses, which pushes the rate higher.</li></ul>", "p": 253}, {"id": "ind-u9-32", "q": "How do the financials of a maritime/shipping company different from a traditional company?", "a": "<ul><li><b>D&amp;A is shown separately</b> on the income statement (rather than buried in COGS or SG&amp;A), so it’s easy to derive EBITDA top-down — after removing non-recurring items.</li><li><b>Taxes are minimal or zero</b>, since companies are typically domiciled in tax-friendly jurisdictions. Specific transport taxes — e.g. tonnage taxes on cargo shipped within some countries — may still appear.</li></ul>", "p": 254}, {"id": "ind-u9-33", "q": "Walk me through a shipping company’s income statement.", "a": "<p><b>Revenue:</b> time charter, spot charter and pool revenue, adjusted for ownership stakes.</p><p><b>Operating expenses:</b></p><ul><li><b>Voyage expenses:</b> port costs, fuel and transport taxes.</li><li><b>Vessel operating expenses:</b> crew, provisions, deck and engine stores, lubricants, insurance, maintenance and repairs.</li><li><b>G&amp;A:</b> salaries, legal costs, stock-based compensation.</li><li><b>D&amp;A</b> on vessels and other fixed assets.</li><li><b>Other:</b> FX gains/losses, goodwill impairments.</li></ul><p><b>Non-operating:</b> net interest expense and other items.</p>", "p": 254}, {"id": "ind-u9-34", "q": "Walk me through a shipping company’s balance sheet.", "a": "<p><b>Assets:</b> mainly vessels and vessels under construction, plus cash.</p><p><b>Liabilities:</b> mainly current and long-term debt. Shipping companies carry little to no working capital.</p><p><b>Equity:</b> common and treasury stock, preferred stock, retained earnings and other comprehensive income.</p>", "p": 254}, {"id": "ind-u9-35", "q": "How do you value a shipping company?", "a": "<ul><li><b>Net asset value (NAV)</b> — the most common.</li><li><b>EV multiples:</b> EV/EBITDA, EV/EBIT.</li><li><b>Equity multiples:</b> price/cash flow, price/NAV.</li></ul>", "p": 255}, {"id": "ind-u9-36", "q": "Walk me through a NAV valuation for a shipping company.", "a": "<ol><li><b>Value the vessels:</b> start with each ship’s “steel” (market) value from trade journal benchmarks — the bulk of the valuation.</li><li><b>Adjust for charters:</b> run a DCF on each vessel’s contracted charter rate vs the forward curve of market rates (typically at ~10%), and add the value of above- or below-market contracts to get a <b>charter-adjusted</b> vessel value.</li><li><b>Add other assets</b> — cash, vessels under construction (at book or projected market value).</li><li><b>Subtract debt</b> and other liabilities.</li></ol><p>The result is NAV-derived equity value — compare with market cap, or per share to get <b>Price/NAV</b>. Trading at a premium to NAV is seen as a positive.</p>", "p": 255}, {"id": "ind-u9-37", "q": "Why might the NAV be preferable to a DCF for shipping companies?", "a": "<p>Shipping balance sheets are full of real assets — vessels — with <b>observable market values</b> from active sale and purchase markets. NAV just restates the balance sheet at fair value. Most industries can’t do that, so they rely on DCFs.</p>", "p": 255}, {"id": "ind-u9-38", "q": "What are some factors that may affect the valuation of a shipping company?", "a": "<p>Valuation depends on daily revenue, charter rates and vessel values, which are driven by two things:</p><ul><li><b>Demand for what’s shipped:</b> crude and refined products, LNG, dry bulk (coal, iron ore, grain) and container trade.</li><li><b>Supply of vessels:</b> ships on the water plus the <b>order book</b> of ships being built.</li></ul><p>Ships take ~18 months to build, so when demand is strong, owners rush to order, and the new supply arrives after a lag — often into a weaker market. Timing purchases and deliveries is critical; owners who bought at the top can end up financially underwater.</p>", "p": 255}]}], "ind-u10": [{"id": "ind-u10-l1", "title": "Oil & gas: sectors, accounting & valuation", "pages": "257–259", "cards": [{"id": "ind-u10-1", "q": "What are the three major subsectors that make up O&G? Tell me a bit about each.", "a": "<p><b>Upstream:</b> exploration &amp; production (E&amp;P) companies, which produce the oil and gas, and oilfield services (OFS) companies, which run drilling rigs and provide seismic and geological data to support them.</p><p><b>Midstream:</b> transporting (pipelines, rail) and storing hydrocarbons (crude and product terminals, gas and NGL storage). Processing gas into natural gas liquids also counts as midstream.</p><p><b>Downstream:</b> refining crude into gasoline (“mogas”), distillates and other products.</p>", "p": 257}, {"id": "ind-u10-2", "q": "How do you value each of the subsectors of O&G?", "a": "<ul><li><b>OFS and refining:</b> like normal companies — EV/EBITDA, P/E.</li><li><b>Midstream:</b> EV/EBITDA, complicated by <b>MLP</b> structures that paid incentive distribution rights (IDRs) to the general partner — that “GP burden” should be counted in net debt. Otherwise, a dividend (distribution) discount model.</li><li><b>E&amp;Ps:</b> EV/EBITDA or EV/EBITDAX, but mainly a <b>NAV model</b> — a long-term DCF (30+ years) that produces reserves down to zero, then adjusts for net debt, non-operating assets, asset retirement obligations, hedges and G&amp;A.</li></ul><p class=\"muted\">Most big MLPs eliminated IDRs or converted to corporations after 2018.</p>", "p": 257}, {"id": "ind-u10-3", "q": "Name a few O&G industry specific multiples beyond the typical multiples.", "a": "<ul><li><b>EV/EBITDAX:</b> EBITDA plus exploration expense (for successful efforts companies), putting them on a par with full cost peers.</li><li><b>EV/DACF:</b> debt-adjusted cash flow = cash from operations + after-tax interest (before or after working capital).</li><li><b>EV/daily production</b> (“EV per flowing barrel”), in BOE or gas-equivalent units.</li><li><b>EV/reserves:</b> usually proved reserves in the US (EV/1P); Canada allows EV/2P.</li><li><b>Acreage value:</b> in many deals undeveloped acreage is the most valuable asset — e.g. Permian deals at roughly $30,000 to $60,000+ per acre (varies by region).</li></ul>", "p": 257}, {"id": "ind-u10-4", "q": "Describe the difference between full cost accounting and successful efforts accounting for E&Ps, and then the impact on the income statements and balance sheets of identical companies.", "a": "<p><b>Successful efforts:</b> geological &amp; geophysical (G&amp;G) costs and <b>exploratory dry holes</b> are expensed immediately (development dry holes are still capitalised).</p><p><b>Full cost:</b> G&amp;G costs and exploratory dry holes are <b>capitalised</b>.</p><p>For identical companies: successful efforts shows <b>lower profits early</b> (more expensed up front) and a smaller asset base. Later, the full cost company shows <b>lower profits</b>, because its larger capitalised base means higher DD&amp;A — and a greater risk of impairments (ceiling test write-downs).</p>", "p": 257}, {"id": "ind-u10-5", "q": "Describe the characteristics that make O&G different from more traditional industries, such as consumer staples.", "a": "<ul><li><b>Commodity pricing:</b> companies are price-takers, with only indirect control via hedging and where they sell.</li><li><b>Depleting assets:</b> fields naturally decline, so companies must keep drilling just to stand still.</li><li><b>Asset-intensive:</b> assets are easily identifiable, so M&amp;A creates little or no goodwill.</li><li><b>Cyclical:</b> severe booms and busts make it hard to forecast.</li><li><b>Reserve disclosures:</b> E&amp;Ps must disclose a standardised value of their reserves (SFAS 69, now ASC 932).</li></ul>", "p": 258}, {"id": "ind-u10-6", "q": "For oil & gas companies, what is the SMOG disclosure requirement?", "a": "<p>The <b>standardised measure of oil and gas</b>: a required disclosure of discounted future net cash flows from <b>proved</b> reserves. Its limitations:</p><ul><li>Uses SEC-prescribed historical average prices and current costs, with no escalation.</li><li>Excludes hedging and G&amp;A.</li><li>Must use a fixed <b>10%</b> discount rate.</li><li>Covers only proved reserves — no probable/possible reserves, and no midstream or refining assets.</li></ul>", "p": 258}, {"id": "ind-u10-7", "q": "Describe the different categories of reserves.", "a": "<ul><li><b>Proved (1P):</b> ~90% probability that at least this much will be recovered.</li><li><b>Proved + probable (2P):</b> ~50% probability.</li><li><b>Proved + probable + possible (3P):</b> ~10% probability.</li></ul>", "p": 258}, {"id": "ind-u10-8", "q": "For reserves, how do 10-Ks under GAAP differ from IFRS filings?", "a": "<p>US 10-Ks report only <b>proved</b> reserves (investor presentations may show 2P/3P). IFRS filings (e.g. in Canada) allow <b>2P</b> reserves to be reported.</p>", "p": 258}, {"id": "ind-u10-9", "q": "What are some differences between the financial statements for an E&P company vs. a regular company?", "a": "<ul><li>Realised and unrealised <b>hedging gains/losses</b>.</li><li><b>DD&amp;A</b> (depletion, depreciation &amp; amortisation) instead of D&amp;A.</li><li><b>Asset retirement obligations</b> — the cost of plugging and abandoning wells and remediating sites.</li><li><b>Exploration expense</b> (for successful efforts companies), from expensed G&amp;G and exploratory dry holes.</li></ul>", "p": 258}, {"id": "ind-u10-10", "q": "What are some metrics that would be looked at in an O&G M&A model?", "a": "<ul><li><b>Accretion/dilution to NAV per share</b> (EPS accretion matters mainly for large companies).</li><li><b>Debt-adjusted per-share growth:</b> production (or reserves) growth per share after converting debt into shares at the current price — neutral to how growth was financed.</li><li><b>Reserves-to-production (R/P):</b> year-end reserves ÷ annual production — how many years of reserves are left.</li><li><b>Production (reserve) replacement ratio:</b> new reserves ÷ production — ideally above 100%, and better from drilling than acquisitions.</li><li><b>F&amp;D cost:</b> finding and development costs ÷ new reserves — the $ cost of adding each barrel (with or without acquisitions).</li></ul>", "p": 258}, {"id": "ind-u10-11", "q": "Would an E&P company be a good candidate for an LBO? Why or why not?", "a": "<p>Generally <b>no</b>. E&amp;Ps have no control over prices, their cash flows are volatile and unpredictable, and their assets deplete and need constant reinvestment — the opposite of the stable cash flows needed to support heavy debt.</p>", "p": 259}, {"id": "ind-u10-12", "q": "What are IDCs, and what type of companies can use them?", "a": "<p><b>Intangible drilling costs</b> — labour, fuel, site preparation and other non-salvageable drilling costs. For tax, independent (non-integrated) producers can deduct them <b>immediately</b>; integrated companies with refining can expense only part (70%) and must amortise the rest.</p>", "p": 259}, {"id": "ind-u10-13", "q": "What is the energy equivalency between a barrel of oil and natural gas in cubic feet?", "a": "<p>About <b>6,000 cubic feet</b> of gas (6 Mcf) per barrel of oil. That’s how you convert between barrels of oil equivalent (BOE) and thousand cubic feet equivalent (Mcfe).</p><p class=\"muted\">This is an energy ratio — on price, a barrel of oil has usually been worth far more than 6 Mcf of gas.</p>", "p": 259}]}]}};
